WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:07.720
How is money created? Where does
it come from? Who benefits?

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:09.640
And what purpose does it serve?

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:13.560
What is a money system? What is the money
behind the money system?

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:20.680
For centuries the mechanics of the money system
have remained hidden from the prying eyes of the populace.

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:28.200
Yet its impact, both on a national and
international level, is perhaps unsurpassed,

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:36.040
for it is the monetary system that provides the foundations
for international dominance and national control.

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:45.040
Today, as these very foundations are being
shaken by crises,

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:51.960
the need for open and honest dialogue on the future
of the monetary system has never been greater.

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:54.680
This economic crisis is like a cancer.

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:59.800
If you just wait and wait, thinking this is going
to go away, just like a cancer it's going to grow,

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:04.520
and it's going to be too late. What
I would say to everybody is, get prepared.

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:09.440
This is not a time right now for wishful thinking
that the government is going to sort things out.

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:13.800
The governments don't rule the world:
Goldman Sachs rules the world.

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:16.720
"We're on the verge of a perfect storm".

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:23.600
In opposition lie corrupt and entrenched interests
that lurk in the corridors of power,

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:29.320
for whom there are no reasons to relinquish
privileges that they feel are justly deserved.

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:36.960
Has he got a reform plan for the NHS? [SHOUT: No!]
Has he got a police reform plan? [No!]

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:39.920
Has he got a plan to cut the deficit? [No!]

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:46.720
Order! Misorder! Order!

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:49.760
Do you trust the government?

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:59.640
Try to calm down and behave like an adult,
and if you can't, if it's beyond you,

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:02.960
leave the chamber. Get out.
We'll manage without you!

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:06.520
"This is the banking fraternities feeding station.

00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:13.560
There's no coincidence that boom and bust became
a real cyclical issue around about the 1700's,

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:17.040
when William Paterson founded the Bank of England.

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:27.520
This is intolerable behaviour as far as the
public is concerned. No, it's not funny!

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:35.720
Only in your mind is it funny. It's not
funny at all, it's disgraceful.

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:39.480
One Solution, Revolution!

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:48.160
The system is inherently unstable as a result of the
international power it provides to the dominant parties,

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:54.040
for at the heart of it lies the idea of;
how can I get something for nothing?

00:02:54.440 --> 00:03:00.760
Statistical analysis has found that every
time an empire begins to near its own demise,

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:03.240
you'll find that its currency will be debased.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:06.880
There is no guide to how this whole system operates.

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:11.520
To give you an example, a researcher at the BBC
working on a Robert Peston documentary

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:17.200
went to the Bank of England and said,
"Can you give me a guide to how money is created?"

00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:19.280
And they just said, "No".

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:24.120
This documentary will investigate and explain
this ever changing system,

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:28.760
and the impact it has both on a
national and international level.

00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:37.920
97% Owned

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:42.400
How is money created?

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:58.320
Notes and coins

00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:09.880
In 2010 the total UK money supply stood at
2.15 trillion pounds.

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:15.440
2.6 % of this total was physical cash, 53.5 billion.

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:23.880
The rest, 2.1 trillion, or 97.4% of the
total money supply was commercial bank money.

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:30.280
The 3% of money is created through the central bank

00:04:30.520 --> 00:04:36.840
and that money essentially, if you created
a £10 note you could sell that to a bank

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:42.480
to put into their ATM and the bank would have
to repay that £10 or buy it for £10.

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:49.560
There would be no interest charged on that money,
but that money is then essentially transferred to the Treasury

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:55.440
and it's a form of fundraising for the government.
It's called seigniorage.

00:04:55.720 --> 00:05:04.120
Seigniorage: Profit made by a government by issuing currency.
The difference between the face value of notes and coins,
and their production costs.

00:05:04.520 --> 00:05:11.280
When the Bank Of England creates a £10 note, it costs
it about 3 or 4 pence to actually print that note

00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:18.960
and it sells it to a high street banks
at face value, so for £10 and the profit,

00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:26.040
the difference between printing the note and actually
selling it for £10 goes directly to the treasury.

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:34.280
So, in effect all the profit that we get on creating
physical money, bank notes, goes to the Treasury

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:41.360
and it reduces how much taxes we have to pay.
Over the last 10 years, that's raised about £18 billion.

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:48.560
In 1948 notes and coins constituted 17% of
the total money supply.

00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:54.480
This was one contributing factor in the government's
ability to finance post-war reconstruction.

00:05:54.920 --> 00:05:57.640
This included the establishment of the NHS.

00:05:59.480 --> 00:06:03.920
In only 60 years notes and coins have shrunk
to less than 3%.

00:06:10.120 --> 00:06:17.400
Prior to 1844 bank notes were created by private banks and
the government did not profit from their creation.

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:23.760
Pre-industrialisation there was multiple forms of money co-existing,

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:31.320
and so the rise of government-sponsored
fiat money is a relatively recent phenomenon.

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:36.360
In the 1840s there was no law to stop banks
from creating their own bank notes.

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:43.880
So they used to issue paper notes as kind of a
representative of what you had in the bank account.

00:06:44.200 --> 00:06:49.320
Instead of you taking your heavy metal coins out of
the bank and then going and paying somebody with them

00:06:49.720 --> 00:06:54.240
you could get your paper which said how much money
you had in the bank and you could give that to somebody

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:57.720
and they could use that to go and get
the heavy metal coins from the banks.

00:06:58.040 --> 00:07:02.200
Now over time these paper
notes became as good as money.

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:06.440
People would use paper notes instead of going
and getting real money from the bank

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:15.040
and obviously as soon as the banks realised that what they
were creating had become the dominant type of money in the economy,

00:07:15.320 --> 00:07:18.480
they realised that by creating more
of it they could generate profits.

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:23.320
They can just print up some new notes
lend it and get the interest on top of them.

00:07:23.720 --> 00:07:29.200
And they did that up until the 1840s. In the
1840s they pushed it just a little bit too far

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:34.560
and that caused inflation, destabilising
the economy. So in 1844,

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:39.560
the Conservative Government of Robert Peel
actually passed a law

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:45.440
that took the power to create money away from
the commercial banks and brought it back to the state.

00:07:45.720 --> 00:07:52.880
So since then the Bank of England has been the only
organisation authorised to create paper notes.

00:07:56.000 --> 00:08:04.880
Since then everything has gone digital and what we now use as money
is the digital numbers that commercial banks can create out of nothing.

00:08:07.080 --> 00:08:13.560
The problem was that they did not include
in that legislation the deposits,

00:08:13.800 --> 00:08:21.520
the demand deposits, held in banks by individuals
or electronic forms of money

00:08:21.800 --> 00:08:29.200
which essentially is what those demand deposits are.
Today most of the money in circulation is electronic money,

00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:36.120
it's bank demand deposits that sit in our accounts.

00:08:36.440 --> 00:08:46.160
So in a way the legislation's got to catch up with the developments in
electronic money and the way that banks actually operate.

00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:50.560
Money held in bank accounts are called demand deposits.

00:08:51.320 --> 00:08:54.680
This is an accounting term the banks
use when they create credit.

00:08:55.680 --> 00:09:03.360
Banks follow the same process when they create loans. All
money held in bank accounts is an accounting entry.

00:09:07.120 --> 00:09:10.400
Commercial bank money

00:09:11.120 --> 00:09:16.800
The reality is now that most money is not
paper and it's not metal coins it's digital.

00:09:17.120 --> 00:09:23.320
It's just numbers in a computer system.
It's your Visa debit card. It's your electronic ATM card.

00:09:24.800 --> 00:09:31.280
It's this - plastic. It's numbers in a computer system,
you move money from one computer system to another.

00:09:31.640 --> 00:09:38.160
It's all a big database and this digital money
is what we are now using to make payments with.

00:09:38.400 --> 00:09:40.560
It's what we actually use to run the economy.

00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:45.480
I think a lot of people in the UK probably
think that the government or the central bank

00:09:46.800 --> 00:09:53.240
is in control of most money in circulation
and issues new money into circulation,

00:09:53.520 --> 00:09:55.720
but that's not the case.

00:09:55.920 --> 00:10:03.240
It's private banks that create the vast majority of new money
in circulation and also decide how it's allocated.

00:10:07.320 --> 00:10:12.320
The official terminology for this accounting
entry is commercial bank money.

00:10:13.320 --> 00:10:17.560
When banks issue loans to the public,
they create new commercial bank money.

00:10:18.640 --> 00:10:22.880
When a customer repays a loan,
commercial bank money is destroyed.

00:10:23.520 --> 00:10:26.200
The banks keep the interest as profit.

00:10:27.720 --> 00:10:30.240
There're a lot of misconceptions about
the way banks work.

00:10:30.520 --> 00:10:38.400
There was a poll done by the Cobden Centre where they
asked people how they thought banks actually operated.

00:10:38.720 --> 00:10:44.520
Around 30% of the public think that when you put
your money into the bank it just stays there and its safe,

00:10:44.920 --> 00:10:50.560
and you can understand why because every child has
a piggy bank where you keep putting money in

00:10:50.920 --> 00:10:54.560
and then when it's a rainy day you smash it
and you take that money out and you spend it.

00:10:54.920 --> 00:10:58.160
So a lot of people keep this idea of banking

00:10:58.400 --> 00:11:02.680
it's somewhere safe to keep your money
so that it's there for whenever you need it.

00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:11.920
Another, the other 60% of people assume that when
 you put your money in, that money is then being
 moved across to somebody who wants to borrow it.

00:11:12.240 --> 00:11:18.200
So you have a pensioner who keeps saving money her
entire life and then her life savings have been lent

00:11:18.520 --> 00:11:22.840
to some young people who want to buy a house.
But actually banks don't work like that.

00:11:23.120 --> 00:11:42.320
"It's basically an accounting trick….banks create money. They don't lend it
…when a bank gives out what is called a loan, it basically pretends
that you have deposited the money… it has to invent the liability…
this is how the money supply is created." (Professor Richard Werner)

00:11:42.320 --> 00:11:49.360
At the moment in the UK money creation and control
is largely in the hands of private banks.

00:11:50.320 --> 00:11:58.480
About 97 to 98% of money that's created
is created as bank "debt money" you could call it,

00:11:59.320 --> 00:12:04.040
when banks issue money into circulation as loans essentially.

00:12:04.920 --> 00:12:07.480
This is a very poorly understood fact.

00:12:08.760 --> 00:12:16.320
It's not a conspiracy theory, it's not a crackpot
theory, it's the way the Bank of England describes the process.

00:12:16.680 --> 00:12:18.600
When banks make loans they create new money.

00:12:18.920 --> 00:12:31.640
…by far the largest role in creating money is played
by the banking sector…When banks make loans they create additional
deposits for those that have borrowed the money.
(Paul Tucker - Deputy Governor of the Bank of England)

00:12:32.000 --> 00:12:35.200
A few economists will realise
the way the money system works

00:12:35.480 --> 00:12:39.480
but if you don't realise the way
that money works and you think that

00:12:39.920 --> 00:12:42.560
everybody saving is going to work well for the economy,

00:12:42.800 --> 00:12:46.040
what really happens once you understand
the way the money system works,

00:12:46.320 --> 00:12:52.160
is that if everybody starts saving the amount of money
in the economy shrinks and we have a recession.

00:12:52.520 --> 00:12:57.520
Most economists don't have this full picture.
They don't understand all the elements of the system.

00:12:57.800 --> 00:13:05.160
They rely on assumptions, on received knowledge
without actually going into the details

00:13:05.800 --> 00:13:11.240
and money is the centre of the economy.
If you don't understand where it comes from,

00:13:11.520 --> 00:13:16.280
who creates it and when it gets created
then how can you understand the entire economy?

00:13:23.120 --> 00:13:28.880
When the vast majority of money that we use now
is not cash but electronic money

00:13:29.200 --> 00:13:33.040
then whoever's creating the electronic money is
getting the proceeds of creating that money

00:13:33.320 --> 00:13:36.840
and obviously creating electronic money is
much more profitable than creating cash

00:13:37.200 --> 00:13:39.680
because you don't have any production cost at all.

00:13:40.040 --> 00:13:46.760
So while we've got £18 billion over the
course of the decade in profit from creating cash,

00:13:47.200 --> 00:13:50.080
the banks have actually created £1.2 trillion.

00:13:51.000 --> 00:13:55.760
Between 1998 and 2007 the UK money supply tripled.

00:13:56.520 --> 00:14:03.360
£1.2 trillion was created by banks,
whilst £18 billion was created by the Treasury.

00:14:06.280 --> 00:14:12.160
A lot of people think when I say this or when you say this
or when Positive Money say this, that we are all a bunch of nutters.

00:14:12.520 --> 00:14:18.600
But on the 9th of March in 2009, the governor of
the Federal Reserve, Ben Bernanke,

00:14:18.920 --> 00:14:25.320
gave the first ever broadcast interview the Governor
of the Central Bank of the United States of America had ever given.

00:14:26.320 --> 00:14:33.400
The day before that he had bailed out AIG,
which is an insurance company not even a bank actually,

00:14:34.400 --> 00:14:36.520
to the tune of about US$160 billion.

00:14:36.720 --> 00:14:43.360
So the journalist says to him: "Now Mr. Bernanke
where did you get $160 billion to bail out AIG?"

00:14:43.800 --> 00:14:46.040
Is that tax money that the Fed is spending?

00:14:46.320 --> 00:14:54.160
It's not tax money. The banks have accounts with the Fed,
much the same way that you have an account in a commercial bank.

00:14:54.600 --> 00:15:00.720
So to lend to a bank we simply use the computer to mark
up the size of the account they have with the Fed.

00:15:01.720 --> 00:15:06.600
So it's much more akin, although not exactly the same,
to printing money than it is to borrowing.

00:15:09.800 --> 00:15:15.000
Banks create new money whenever they extend credit,
buy existing assets,

00:15:15.240 --> 00:15:20.120
or make payments on their own account,
which mostly involves expanding their assets.

00:15:22.160 --> 00:15:26.280
When a bank buys securities,
such as a Corporate or Government Bond

00:15:26.760 --> 00:15:32.880
it adds the bond to its assets and increases
the company's bank deposits by the corresponding amount.

00:15:35.040 --> 00:15:41.240
New commercial bank money enters circulation when people
spend the credit that has been granted to them by banks.

00:15:42.960 --> 00:15:52.680
I found that talking on the door step from
August 2009 around to the general election,

00:15:54.560 --> 00:16:00.040
knocking on the doors, is that when we tried
to explain how the money system works,

00:16:01.360 --> 00:16:10.400
there's an almost in-built refusal of people to accept
that such a bizarre situation could actually exist.

00:16:11.160 --> 00:16:17.920
"Ah no, it can't possibly. What do you mean? It can't...
banks can't...banks don't create money out of thin air.

00:16:18.240 --> 00:16:21.600
That's ridiculous. They can't do that.
They lend out their depositors' money."

00:16:21.880 --> 00:16:26.920
Most people have an idea of how money is.
They are used to their own way of handling money

00:16:27.760 --> 00:16:36.560
and they try and implement their own idea of how their
small household economy works into the national economy.

00:16:36.840 --> 00:16:40.040
And of course it just doesn't work out at all.

00:16:40.760 --> 00:16:44.920
By 2008 the outstanding loan portfolio of
bank created credit,

00:16:45.440 --> 00:16:50.080
also known as commercial bank money,
stood at over 2 trillion.

00:16:51.320 --> 00:16:57.560
As recently as 1982 the ratio of notes and
coins to bank deposits was 1:12.

00:16:58.760 --> 00:17:03.040
By 2010 the ratio had risen to 1:37.

00:17:04.000 --> 00:17:10.320
That is for every pound of Treasury created money
there were 37 pounds of bank created money.

00:17:12.600 --> 00:17:15.640
In the 10 years prior to the 2007 crisis,

00:17:16.160 --> 00:17:21.880
the UK commercial bank money supply expanded
by between 7% to 10% every year.

00:17:22.480 --> 00:17:27.880
A growth rate of 7% is the equivalent of doubling
the money supply every 10 years.

00:17:29.800 --> 00:17:32.880
The amount of money they're creating out of
nothing is just incredible,

00:17:33.160 --> 00:17:35.400
1.2 trillion in the last 10 years.

00:17:36.840 --> 00:17:41.880
That money is being distributed according to
the priorities of the banking sector,

00:17:42.240 --> 00:17:44.160
not the priorities of society.

00:17:45.600 --> 00:17:55.440
The banking sector itself grew from 1980 $2.5
trillion to $40 trillion by assets.

00:17:55.960 --> 00:18:01.280
In 1980, global bank assets were worth 20 times the
then global economy.

00:18:01.960 --> 00:18:06.040
By 2006 they were worth 75 times, according to the UN.

00:18:07.920 --> 00:18:13.800
As the following chart shows, total bank assets
of UK banks as a percentage of GDP

00:18:14.160 --> 00:18:23.200
remained relatively stable at 50-60% up to the end
of the 1960s. After that they shot up dramatically.

00:18:23.560 --> 00:18:28.560
And the real money in the world to be made
today is not by producing anything at all.

00:18:28.880 --> 00:18:33.080
It's simply by forms of speculating - basically
making money from money.

00:18:33.360 --> 00:18:42.280
That's the most profitable and by far and away the biggest
form of economic activity that exists in the world today.

00:18:43.960 --> 00:18:48.120
Today, banks are no longer restricted by how
much they can lend,

00:18:48.960 --> 00:18:58.560
and as such, how much new credit they can create out of nothing.
They are restricted solely by their own willingness to lend.

00:19:01.120 --> 00:19:04.360
The issue with allowing banks to create money
- there's two main issues.

00:19:04.640 --> 00:19:07.960
Firstly - the fact that they create this money
when they make loans,

00:19:08.320 --> 00:19:14.120
so it guarantees that we have to borrow all
our money for the economy from the banks.

00:19:15.240 --> 00:19:20.320
As such, to have a healthy growing economy,
the Government needs to put in place strategies

00:19:20.560 --> 00:19:23.080
to allow for ever-increasing debt.

00:19:24.240 --> 00:19:30.920
The only way the Government can create additional
purchasing power is by getting itself and us into more debt.

00:19:32.760 --> 00:19:39.600
The second big issue with allowing the banks to create
money is that they have the incentive to always create more.

00:19:39.960 --> 00:19:42.080
They create more money if they issue a loan.

00:19:42.360 --> 00:19:46.880
They get the bonuses, the commissions and the
incentives to lend as much as possible.

00:19:47.160 --> 00:19:50.120
You have to develop a sales culture.
What did they do?

00:19:50.560 --> 00:19:55.760
They recruited an amazing guy, a lovely guy,
Andy Hornby, who came from Asda

00:19:56.280 --> 00:20:00.240
to turn the bank into a supermarket retailing operation.

00:20:00.680 --> 00:20:04.040
If you trust bankers to control the money supply,

00:20:04.360 --> 00:20:08.080
the money supply will just grow and
grow and grow, as will the level of debt,

00:20:08.360 --> 00:20:14.000
until the point where it crashes, when some people
can't repay the debt and then they'll stop lending.

00:20:15.760 --> 00:20:19.640
You hear politicians and journalists saying
We've been living beyond our means.

00:20:19.920 --> 00:20:25.520
We've become dependent on debt. We need to reign
in our spending and live within our means.

00:20:26.240 --> 00:20:28.040
It's not possible in the current system.

00:20:29.640 --> 00:20:33.800
The reason why everyone is in debt now is not
because they have been recklessly borrowing.

00:20:34.360 --> 00:20:39.480
We haven't borrowed all this money from an army of
pensioners who've been saving up their whole lives.

00:20:39.960 --> 00:20:44.400
Money in the current system is debt.
It's created when the banks make loans.

00:20:44.960 --> 00:20:48.680
So the only way, in the current system,
that we can have any money in the economy,

00:20:48.960 --> 00:20:54.360
the only way we can have money for business to trade,
is if we've borrowed it all from the banks.

00:20:56.760 --> 00:21:01.480
And it's the very opposite of what the
Tory Party is arguing today,

00:21:01.840 --> 00:21:06.120
which is that you have to create savings
before you can help the National Health Service.

00:21:06.760 --> 00:21:10.160
And it's because economists have completely
confused those things,

00:21:10.440 --> 00:21:14.560
both in monetary policy terms,
but also in economic thinking,

00:21:14.800 --> 00:21:21.080
and because most people still harbour the old fashioned
view that you need savings before you can invest,

00:21:21.480 --> 00:21:24.200
that we have the mess that we're in today.

00:21:24.560 --> 00:21:30.400
Now, one of the reasons why we find it difficult to
understand the banking system and credit creation,

00:21:30.640 --> 00:21:37.320
is that we leave school without any money and we go
and get a job working as an apprentice to a plumber.

00:21:37.880 --> 00:21:42.360
We work really hard all month and at the end
of the month somebody puts money in our bank,

00:21:42.640 --> 00:21:48.040
and so for us the logic is: you work and then
you get money, you get savings.

00:21:48.360 --> 00:21:53.880
In reality you would never have got that job
if credit hadn't been created in the first instance.

00:21:54.240 --> 00:21:59.960
It's a really important conceptual misunderstanding

00:22:00.960 --> 00:22:05.160
and it isn't something that the public just is
guilty of. Economists don't understand this stuff.

00:22:05.440 --> 00:22:08.040
Money doesn't come out of economic activity.

00:22:08.440 --> 00:22:13.440
A lot of people I've come across kind of
assume that if you have got businesses

00:22:13.760 --> 00:22:20.440
and you've got people doing things, that somehow
money emerges out of the process of people doing things,

00:22:20.960 --> 00:22:24.520
making things and growing things,
selling things and producing things,

00:22:24.960 --> 00:22:30.360
that somehow money just emerges. It's not.
It's like oiling a car. You have to put it in.

00:22:30.760 --> 00:22:36.000
When I see David Cameron talking about how
we need an economy not based on debt,

00:22:36.240 --> 00:22:41.240
but we need an economy based on savings, he just
doesn't know what he's saying. It's ridiculous.

00:22:41.480 --> 00:22:47.880
It's absolutely absurd and it shows his complete
lack of understanding of how our money system actually works.

00:22:48.160 --> 00:22:53.040
What he is essentially saying is that
We need an economy with no money.

00:22:53.360 --> 00:22:57.440
If everyone was saving we'd have mass disappearing of money,

00:22:57.760 --> 00:23:02.320
which is essentially what a bank write-off is
- people defaulting on their debt -

00:23:02.760 --> 00:23:05.600
which essentially is just money disappearing.

00:23:06.040 --> 00:23:10.200
But if people weren't taking on the debt
then it's just such a joke.

00:23:11.360 --> 00:23:18.400
It's such an amateur understanding of how our economy works
and how the monetary system works and how money is actually created.

00:23:18.760 --> 00:23:22.440
So I really do get a laugh out of watching
what people are actually saying.

00:23:22.760 --> 00:23:26.880
They are all just regurgitating what they have learnt
off each other and you just hear the same things

00:23:27.160 --> 00:23:32.960
and it just really gets on my nerves
when I hear people talking about

00:23:33.760 --> 00:23:38.760
'Yeah, we need more regulations, we need to regulate
the way banks are actually and the bonuses'

00:23:39.040 --> 00:23:45.200
It's all just one big smoke screen and working on all the
symptoms of a greater disease which is really

00:23:45.440 --> 00:23:49.840
you need to look at the money system - the
way money is created.

00:23:50.160 --> 00:23:54.960
If we don't want any debt then we're essentially saying
We don't want any money and we want a moneyless economy

00:23:55.240 --> 00:23:58.560
with the exception of the 3% that's created debt free.

00:23:59.200 --> 00:24:01.000
You know, it's a paradox under the current system.

00:24:01.240 --> 00:24:06.440
If we as the public go into further debt
then that's going to put more money into the economy

00:24:06.760 --> 00:24:12.360
and we're going to have a boom. When you have a boom,
it's easier to borrow, so people get into even more debt.

00:24:12.560 --> 00:24:18.920
And eventually this cycle continues. It gets easier
and easier to get into debt until some people get over-indebted

00:24:19.160 --> 00:24:22.480
and then they default. They can't re-pay their mortgage.

00:24:23.720 --> 00:24:30.560
That's what happened first in sub-prime America.
And then it brings through a wave of defaults,

00:24:30.840 --> 00:24:34.560
which will ripple across the entire economy.
The banks go insolvent.

00:24:34.840 --> 00:24:39.080
Then we're into a financial crisis and then
the banks stop lending.

00:24:39.360 --> 00:24:46.920
They were excessively lending in the boom and then
they stop lending and that makes the recession even worse.

00:24:47.360 --> 00:24:52.600
People lose their jobs and then they become even
more dependent on debt just to survive, basically.

00:24:53.160 --> 00:24:57.680
You know we have a system where we have to
borrow in order to have an economy.

00:24:58.040 --> 00:25:03.400
We have to be in debt to the banks. That
guarantees a massive profit for the banks.

00:25:05.360 --> 00:25:07.240
This is the boom-bust cycle.

00:25:10.360 --> 00:25:15.200
And I've said before, Mr Deputy Speaker, no
return to boom and bust.

00:25:21.240 --> 00:25:23.640
Net bank lending must forever increase.

00:25:26.240 --> 00:25:31.360
We are paying interest on every single pound.
Even if you think the money belongs to you,

00:25:31.640 --> 00:25:33.640
somebody somewhere is paying interest on that money.

00:25:33.920 --> 00:25:38.080
The banking system has such a huge impact on the world,

00:25:38.320 --> 00:25:41.960
but only because it supplies our nation's money supply.

00:25:42.440 --> 00:25:46.720
We have to protect them. We have to subsidise them.
We have to allow them to continue

00:25:46.960 --> 00:25:52.120
because the disaster of a bank collapse
affects us all in a huge way.

00:25:52.440 --> 00:25:58.800
Anyone who says that we shouldn't have bailed out the banks
doesn't quite understand the nature of our monetary system.

00:25:59.240 --> 00:26:02.240
That's like eliminating a huge chunk of our money.

00:26:02.840 --> 00:26:08.520
But also bailing out the banks is perpetuating a system
which is never going to work anyway.

00:26:09.040 --> 00:26:14.720
So whatever we do we are always going to have
this cycle until we separate how money is created

00:26:15.040 --> 00:26:17.480
and the activities of banking.
Then the banks could do as they wish.

00:26:17.760 --> 00:26:19.840
They'd be a normal business like everyone else.

00:26:20.120 --> 00:26:26.840
There's a major democratic issue here as well.
You have these private profit-seeking banks

00:26:27.160 --> 00:26:31.760
creating up to £200 billion a year and
pumping that into the economy wherever they want,

00:26:32.120 --> 00:26:37.960
basically, wherever it suits them, whether
they're pumping it into these toxic derivatives,

00:26:38.240 --> 00:26:42.560
or putting money into housing bubbles, just
making housing more expensive.

00:26:42.920 --> 00:26:48.960
£200 billion in 2007 of new money coming
into the economy, created out of nothing

00:26:49.440 --> 00:26:53.640
and where that gets spent determines
the shape of our economy effectively.

00:26:54.240 --> 00:26:57.960
So if we are going to allow anybody
to create new money out of nothing,

00:26:58.240 --> 00:27:01.760
then we should at least have some democratic
control over how that money's used.

00:27:02.040 --> 00:27:09.320
I mean, would we rather have had that money used for health care,
or to deal with some of the environmental issues or to reduce poverty,

00:27:09.560 --> 00:27:14.840
or would we rather have it to make houses more expensive
so none of us can afford to live in a house.

00:27:18.840 --> 00:27:27.520
You can see it as a subsidy, a special super
subsidy to the banks, for the right to create money,

00:27:28.440 --> 00:27:33.880
which should be for the benefit of the public
and spent through a democratic process.

00:27:36.440 --> 00:27:39.640
Central bank reserve currency

00:27:40.640 --> 00:27:46.080
There's also another form of money, which
is effectively an electronic version of cash

00:27:46.640 --> 00:27:52.360
and it's a type of money that the commercial banks use
themselves to make payments between each other.

00:27:53.040 --> 00:27:56.600
The high street banks don't want to be
carrying around huge quantities of money

00:27:57.040 --> 00:28:02.760
because it's dangerous, inconvenient and expensive.
You have to hire security guards for that type of money.

00:28:03.240 --> 00:28:08.120
So what they do is they pay each other
in what is an electronic version of cash

00:28:09.240 --> 00:28:17.920
which in the industry is known as Central Bank Reserves.
They keep this electronic cash in accounts at the Bank of England.

00:28:18.840 --> 00:28:22.560
But as a member of the public you can't access this electronic cash,

00:28:22.840 --> 00:28:24.880
you can't get an account with the Bank of England.

00:28:25.240 --> 00:28:33.600
What they do is they effectively sell this central bank
money to the banks and they do this by creating it out of nothing

00:28:34.240 --> 00:28:39.960
and using this money to pay for bonds,
to buy bonds from the high street banks.

00:28:41.040 --> 00:28:45.280
So, the high street bank will come along with a bond
which is effectively government debt

00:28:45.840 --> 00:28:48.720
and it will give it to the Bank of England
and in return the Bank of England

00:28:49.000 --> 00:28:53.200
will type some new numbers into the
bank's account at the Bank of England.

00:28:53.520 --> 00:28:56.600
So effectively they are creating central bank
reserves out of nothing.

00:28:57.080 --> 00:28:59.840
The Bank of England creates Central Bank Reserves

00:29:00.080 --> 00:29:05.240
by increasing the available credit in the
settlement bank's account with the Bank of England.

00:29:05.880 --> 00:29:12.280
The settlement bank in return posts bonds,
or sells assets as collateral for the reserves.

00:29:14.520 --> 00:29:18.880
A total of 46 banks hold Central Reserve Accounts
at the Bank of England.

00:29:19.840 --> 00:29:28.480
Smaller or foreign banks hold accounts with one of these
46 banks to allow them to accept or make payments in pounds sterling.

00:29:30.040 --> 00:29:38.040
Prior to March 2009, the Bank of England would ask each of the
major settlement banks how much reserve currency they needed.

00:29:38.720 --> 00:29:45.880
The settlement banks would then swap a bond for the reserve
currency and agree to repurchase the bond for a specific amount

00:29:46.440 --> 00:29:52.640
at a specified future date. The settlement banks
would then receive interest

00:29:52.920 --> 00:29:57.880
at base or policy rate for the central bank reserves they held.

00:29:58.720 --> 00:30:04.000
Since the crisis, settlement banks central reserves
have shot up dramatically.

00:30:11.960 --> 00:30:15.200
Significance of central bank reserves

00:30:17.360 --> 00:30:22.160
When bank customers transfer funds from their
account to another person's account,

00:30:22.560 --> 00:30:25.320
a process called Intra-Day Clearing occurs.

00:30:26.440 --> 00:30:30.680
The amount of central reserve currency Bank
A has at the Bank of England

00:30:31.040 --> 00:30:35.480
is reduced by the corresponding amount
that Bank B receives.

00:30:35.960 --> 00:30:39.520
This is the importance of central
reserve currency to banks.

00:30:40.560 --> 00:30:47.520
Before the credit crisis, if a bank was short of central
reserves at the Bank of England to meet its obligations,

00:30:48.360 --> 00:30:52.880
then the bank would have to loan reserves
from other banks with interest.

00:30:54.680 --> 00:31:02.560
Only central bank reserve currency is moved,
commercial bank money is simply deducted and added.

00:31:02.960 --> 00:31:08.600
If you sell something on eBay, you know that that deal
is not complete until you get some money put into your account.

00:31:08.840 --> 00:31:13.560
Most people actually want to see the money in their
account before they're happy to close on a deal.

00:31:13.840 --> 00:31:18.800
Now the banks are pretty much the same, but they want
to see the money in their account at the Bank of England

00:31:19.040 --> 00:31:20.640
before they consider a deal complete.

00:31:21.040 --> 00:31:26.360
So for example, if you are buying a house
from somebody who banks with a different bank

00:31:26.760 --> 00:31:29.640
then what'll happen after you've spent
a quarter of a million on a house

00:31:29.960 --> 00:31:36.680
is you'll tell your bank to transfer some money to the
house seller's bank and what the bank will do

00:31:37.040 --> 00:31:43.240
is actually instruct the Bank of England
to move £250,000 from their account at the Bank of England

00:31:43.560 --> 00:31:50.640
to the bank of the house seller. And that money will actually
move across between the accounts at the Bank of England.

00:31:51.760 --> 00:31:57.280
When that money has moved across, then the banks
will consider that that payment has been settled.

00:31:58.360 --> 00:32:02.960
They don't really deal in the kind of money
that we have in our accounts,

00:32:03.200 --> 00:32:08.160
they deal in this special money that can only
be used at the central bank.

00:32:09.760 --> 00:32:16.200
There are millions of people across the country,
all transferring money to each other using only a few major banks.

00:32:17.360 --> 00:32:24.400
These banks can keep a tally on their computer systems and usually
many of the movements cancel each other out at the end of the day.

00:32:25.960 --> 00:32:36.720
The five major banks - RBS, Lloyds, HSBC, Barclays
and Santander - hold over 85% of all deposits.

00:32:40.160 --> 00:32:47.400
As there are a limited number of banks in the system, the central
reserve money can only be moved around them in a closed loop.

00:32:47.840 --> 00:32:53.280
The money is just circulating through this system
over and over again and if you think about it,

00:32:53.560 --> 00:32:59.520
a one pound coin could be used to make a billion pounds
of payments if it was circulated a billion times.

00:32:59.960 --> 00:33:03.440
And that's effectively the system that you have now,
is you have a small pool of real money

00:33:03.760 --> 00:33:09.840
that's just going round and round the system and it's being
used to make a huge quantity of payments on our behalf.

00:33:10.240 --> 00:33:15.880
Just before the crisis there was only
20 billion in the accounts at the central bank.

00:33:18.120 --> 00:33:23.600
If they don't have enough of this central bank money,
then effectively they can't make payments

00:33:23.920 --> 00:33:27.640
and if that happens then pretty quickly
the entire system seizes up.

00:33:27.920 --> 00:33:33.240
So the Bank of England has the responsibility of making sure
there's enough of this money in the system.

00:33:35.600 --> 00:33:41.520
The requirements for banks to hold a specific
amount of reserves has changed many times since 1947.

00:33:42.520 --> 00:33:50.560
At that time, banks needed to hold a minimum ratio of 32%
of reserves, cash or Treasury Bonds to deposits.

00:33:51.640 --> 00:34:00.240
In 2006, the Corridor System was introduced, in which banks
could set their own reserve targets each month.

00:34:02.720 --> 00:34:09.040
The rules changed again in March 2009 when
the Bank of England introduced quantitative easing.

00:34:09.600 --> 00:34:15.720
Quantitative Easing in effect, gives settlement
banks the central reserve currency for free.

00:34:18.520 --> 00:34:24.600
The Central Reserve Currency is what is referred to
as the real money in the fractional reserve model

00:34:25.000 --> 00:34:28.680
but the fact is banks can have as much of this as they want.

00:34:29.000 --> 00:34:34.120
And Central Reserve Currency itself is a form
of fiat money which is backed by nothing.

00:34:35.160 --> 00:34:39.240
As a consequence there is no longer
a meaningful fractional reserve.

00:34:46.920 --> 00:34:50.000
A short history of money

00:34:51.160 --> 00:34:53.920
If you look over the history of the last 150 years or so,

00:34:54.240 --> 00:35:00.520
you start off with a development of a gold standard
that really comes to the fore in the 1880s/1890s

00:35:00.800 --> 00:35:05.520
where essentially countries peg themselves
to a particular defined value of gold

00:35:05.840 --> 00:35:10.200
and then they have an agreement to fix
that value, to hold that value,

00:35:10.480 --> 00:35:13.520
and to trade gold amongst themselves to
make sure the balances are all there

00:35:13.800 --> 00:35:21.640
and also to try and restrict or expand or contract activity
in their own economies to make sure that the balance,

00:35:21.920 --> 00:35:27.480
that particular fixed price, is maintained.
That disintegrates after the First World War.

00:35:27.920 --> 00:35:33.400
This is where the whole thing breaks apart, a very major
dislocation in the international monetary system at that point,

00:35:33.720 --> 00:35:38.400
not really resolved until you get Bretton Woods
agreements at the end of the Second World War

00:35:38.720 --> 00:35:41.920
in which everything is pegged to the dollar
and the dollar is pegged to the gold.

00:35:42.160 --> 00:35:44.920
So you are kind of one removed from the gold backing

00:35:45.200 --> 00:35:51.120
or saying that there is a definite you know sort of
solid commodity money behind the paper money and the credit money

00:35:51.400 --> 00:35:54.320
that we are all using over here.
You are kind of one removed from it.

00:35:54.560 --> 00:36:01.440
After Hiroshima, Tokyo wondered when the next
atom bomb would fall. They did not wonder long.

00:36:07.720 --> 00:36:16.600
In 1944, at Bretton Woods, the US and the UK began
to negotiate how to govern the world economy,

00:36:16.920 --> 00:36:21.040
the world monetary system and came
up with the World Bank and the IMF

00:36:21.320 --> 00:36:25.760
and a series of other institutions
designed to manage the global currency

00:36:26.120 --> 00:36:30.240
and there was still a gold standard, but this
gold standard was going to be tied to the dollar.

00:36:30.520 --> 00:36:35.440
All of the world's gold had moved from London to Fort Knox,

00:36:36.120 --> 00:36:39.800
and all of the world's currencies were tied to the dollar.

00:36:40.520 --> 00:36:46.400
This system was designed to manage the sorts of
imbalances, to avoid credit crunches,

00:36:46.680 --> 00:36:54.840
or for countries, credit crunches are known as balance of trade deficits
i.e. when they can't pay their bills and their currency collapses.

00:36:55.120 --> 00:37:04.480
The currencies were managed and the system was stable,
as long as the Americans played the role of oversight.

00:37:05.040 --> 00:37:08.800
Now, who knows the great story
about how that all came to an end?

00:37:09.920 --> 00:37:16.280
The quantity of money that was needed to pay for the
Vietnam War, that's exactly what I was trying to get at.

00:37:16.560 --> 00:37:18.040
Oil shocks were another one.

00:37:18.320 --> 00:37:22.960
That meant that the Americans were no longer respecting their role
or playing their role governing the monetary system.

00:37:23.200 --> 00:37:27.280
They were inflating the value of their own
currency that ostensibly was meant to be tied,

00:37:27.560 --> 00:37:32.000
tied to gold and to every other currency.
So what did the French do?

00:37:32.440 --> 00:37:36.920
The French were a little bit worried that
President Nixon wasn't entirely honest.

00:37:38.840 --> 00:37:48.040
And they were worried that precisely what we described, that Nixon
was printing money when he shouldn't have been, was going on.

00:37:48.360 --> 00:37:53.080
And they were worried there wasn't enough gold
to honour the exchange rate of the French Franc,

00:37:53.360 --> 00:38:00.040
so they sent a gunboat to New York harbour to ever
so politely ask for our gold back please.

00:38:01.360 --> 00:38:09.120
Did they get their gold back? Go on, guess! They didn't.
And the Bretton Woods system came to an end.

00:38:11.160 --> 00:38:14.880
And this is the point at which we enter
the modern era of the financial system.

00:38:15.160 --> 00:38:24.960
Fiat money: A medium of exchange, which the issuer
does not promise to redeem in a commodity,
and is based on confidence.

00:38:25.440 --> 00:38:32.120
Historically, money creation was pegged to a commodity,
often gold, but today it is pegged to nothing.

00:38:33.040 --> 00:38:41.520
Which means there is nothing backing our money.
This piece of paper is just a piece of paper.

00:38:43.800 --> 00:38:50.360
Where does this leave us? If money is based
on nothing, why do we think it has any value?

00:38:53.000 --> 00:38:58.320
Sorry? Because we can still go and exchange
it. What? Somebody else was going to shout.

00:38:59.000 --> 00:39:05.280
Great little Latin fact, the word for credit
comes from? belief. Correct.

00:39:06.800 --> 00:39:11.240
Credere = to believe

00:39:13.760 --> 00:39:19.880
Since the collapse of the dollar gold standard in 1971
and the deregulation of the financial system,

00:39:20.360 --> 00:39:22.760
money creation has grown exponentially.

00:39:24.680 --> 00:39:37.320
The World Economic Forum meeting in Davos at the present time
have called on a need for the credit within the economy,

00:39:37.720 --> 00:39:44.280
the global economy, to be expanded by US$100 trillion.

00:39:47.920 --> 00:39:55.600
A trillion is 12 noughts so 100 trillion,
if you want to imagine is a 1 followed by 14 noughts.

00:39:56.400 --> 00:39:59.200
They believe this credit expansion will create a boom

00:39:59.600 --> 00:40:03.080
because there is now more money in
the economy with which to make investments.

00:40:04.120 --> 00:40:09.240
It's fascinating this emergence of digital currencies,
how it's transformed everything really.

00:40:09.920 --> 00:40:17.640
Because it just completely unleashed private banks
to dominate and create the money system that works for them

00:40:18.000 --> 00:40:20.760
and works for the people who run private banks.

00:40:21.520 --> 00:40:24.200
Growth and inflation

00:40:24.920 --> 00:40:29.320
If you want a growing economy under the current
set-up we have to have growing debt.

00:40:31.000 --> 00:40:33.840
This is something very, very few people really understand

00:40:34.200 --> 00:40:38.520
- especially not the politicians who are managing
the economy - which is a scary thought.

00:40:38.800 --> 00:40:45.640
GDP Gross Domestic Product:
The market value of all final goods and services
produced in a country in a given period.

00:40:46.720 --> 00:40:52.520
As the money supply grows more money is available
which can be invested in productive avenues.

00:40:53.600 --> 00:40:57.520
However it can also be used to gamble
and drive up asset prices.

00:40:59.400 --> 00:41:04.880
An increase in the money supply =
A likely relative increase in economic activity.

00:41:07.600 --> 00:41:10.920
The effects of rapid credit expansion

00:41:13.240 --> 00:41:19.560
Inflation is a rise in the general level of the prices
of goods and services in an economy over a period of time.

00:41:21.040 --> 00:41:26.680
When the general price level rises each unit
of currency buys fewer goods and services.

00:41:27.920 --> 00:41:30.680
As the money supply grows and there is more currency available,

00:41:31.320 --> 00:41:34.600
more money is available for investment
which can lead to growth,

00:41:35.400 --> 00:41:42.760
but more money is also available for purchases
of goods and speculation which leads to inflation.

00:41:44.400 --> 00:41:53.080
Essentially, inflation is what happens when
too much money is chasing too few goods and services,

00:41:53.400 --> 00:41:59.480
so there is too much money for the
actual output of the economy.

00:42:02.720 --> 00:42:10.640
In the seven years between the years 2000
and 2007 the money supply doubled and the central bank,

00:42:10.960 --> 00:42:14.640
the Bank of England was under the impression
at this time that they had it under control

00:42:14.960 --> 00:42:17.760
because they were saying that
prices weren't going up that much.

00:42:18.160 --> 00:42:22.400
Of course they were only looking at prices
in your local corner shop.

00:42:22.640 --> 00:42:28.400
They weren't looking at the price of housing and housing
is the biggest expenditure that most people will make.

00:42:28.720 --> 00:42:35.760
Many western countries heavily subsidise
agricultural production, which has the
effect of keeping prices and inflation low.

00:42:36.200 --> 00:42:44.080
Increasing house prices, it may make you feel
like you're becoming wealthier,

00:42:44.560 --> 00:42:50.680
but as your wealth increases the effect is that your
children's wealth is actually decreasing.

00:42:51.080 --> 00:42:59.960
So in fact there is no net gain in wealth because your children
are going to have to pay even more when they want to buy a house.

00:43:00.560 --> 00:43:04.640
So in effect there is no net increase.
They are going to have to earn even more.

00:43:04.960 --> 00:43:07.360
They are going to have to go into even more debt.

00:43:07.960 --> 00:43:15.840
So rising house prices do not create
additional net GDP value to the economy.

00:43:16.760 --> 00:43:22.280
Actually what they do is they re-distribute wealth
towards those people who already have houses

00:43:22.560 --> 00:43:28.240
i.e. wealthier people and remove it from poorer
people who can't afford to get on the housing ladder.

00:43:28.560 --> 00:43:35.320
So it's another example of a very regressive policy
to allow house prices to simply inflate.

00:43:35.640 --> 00:43:41.040
It makes everybody feel like things are going well
and people spend money on other stuff,

00:43:41.320 --> 00:43:45.120
they take equity out of their houses but it's not creating new jobs.

00:43:45.440 --> 00:43:51.080
It's not enhancing the quality of the economy.
It's not helping our balance of trade.

00:43:51.320 --> 00:43:55.440
It's not helping the public deficit.
It's a zero sum game.

00:43:58.280 --> 00:44:06.240
As of August 2011, 85.5% of consumer bank
lending was secured as mortgages on dwellings.

00:44:06.680 --> 00:44:10.680
If you have somebody creating money that
can only be spent on one thing,

00:44:11.000 --> 00:44:14.080
which is housing then the price
of that thing is going to go up.

00:44:14.360 --> 00:44:19.160
Between 2000 and 2010 they created
over a trillion pounds of new money

00:44:19.480 --> 00:44:25.800
- £500 billion just in the three years before the
crisis. That's why house prices went up they way they were.

00:44:26.040 --> 00:44:28.200
There's nothing special about houses.

00:44:28.440 --> 00:44:31.440
It was just all this funny money
being pumped into that market.

00:44:31.640 --> 00:44:38.720
If money is spent into the economy a lot of
money goes into houses for example into mortgages

00:44:40.000 --> 00:44:48.600
- that's an increase in the amount of money in the economy -
without a corresponding increase in activity in output, in GDP.

00:44:48.800 --> 00:44:55.880
It's non-GDP based spending.
That's what causes inflation.

00:44:56.480 --> 00:45:02.080
In the UK we've had it in spades.
We've had this massive housing boom.

00:45:02.680 --> 00:45:05.920
The main cause for the housing boom, in my opinion,

00:45:06.280 --> 00:45:12.160
is the huge amount of speculative credit created
by the banks to go into houses.

00:45:12.480 --> 00:45:19.680
If houses were cheaper, they would be easier
to build. More of them would be built.

00:45:19.960 --> 00:45:24.400
There would be less huge houses,
with hardly any people in them.

00:45:24.680 --> 00:45:30.800
London would not be the centre of
a kind of very rich speculative orgy,

00:45:31.080 --> 00:45:36.320
where all the richest people in the world want to get
a property in London, because it's seen as a great asset.

00:45:36.680 --> 00:45:41.280
Houses would be seen as places to live primarily,
rather than seen as places to invest.

00:45:42.080 --> 00:45:48.360
The important thing to think about is, if you are a bank
and you've got to make a loan, you have choices.

00:45:48.680 --> 00:45:58.040
You can give that loan to a small business and you'll know
that the risk to you of that loan failing, defaulting,

00:45:58.280 --> 00:46:02.600
is actually quite high, because that small
business, the owners of that business,

00:46:02.840 --> 00:46:10.400
have limited liability, which means if that business
goes bust you as a bank get nothing back essentially.

00:46:11.120 --> 00:46:19.640
So that's kind of high risk, compared to loaning your money to
somebody with some collateral, with a house behind them, like a mortgage.

00:46:19.880 --> 00:46:26.960
So there's a simple incentive for banks to prefer
putting money into housing than into a small business.

00:46:27.280 --> 00:46:32.600
Now that's a real problem if you
widen that out across a whole economy,

00:46:33.000 --> 00:46:39.040
because it means there's an incentive to put money
into speculative rather than productive investment.

00:46:39.280 --> 00:46:44.840
So again, we have to think about how we create
our monetary system that is more balanced

00:46:45.080 --> 00:46:49.080
between those two kinds of
speculative and productive investment.

00:46:49.480 --> 00:46:54.080
The government is showing enormous
reluctance to regulate the housing market

00:46:54.440 --> 00:46:59.440
and to again regulate the amount of money
that banks put into houses.

00:46:59.720 --> 00:47:06.080
We don't decide who creates credit for what.  No. We leave
that to a couple of chaps in a bank to decide basically.

00:47:08.760 --> 00:47:12.080
A short history of bubbles

00:47:13.840 --> 00:47:20.360
A bubble occurs when there is very high inflation in the price
of a specific good or service over a short period of time.

00:47:20.600 --> 00:47:23.560
The first recorded bubble was the tulip bubble of 1637.

00:47:23.800 --> 00:47:30.440
The idea of the tulips and their relevance is
that you saw the first ever financial bubble and crash.

00:47:30.960 --> 00:47:39.560
The craze for tulips - black tulips being a mythical ideal
of what somebody could genetically engineer

00:47:39.840 --> 00:47:47.000
through cultivation after many generations -
became a mania in the Netherlands in the 1630s.

00:47:47.360 --> 00:47:51.360
What they didn't realise was that many of
the very, very rare patterns on tulips bulbs

00:47:51.600 --> 00:47:58.480
were caused by a virus and weren't genetic at all.
But they traded them to the extent that tulip got to the point

00:47:58.800 --> 00:48:04.800
where they were worth ten times the average
annual salary of a person working in the Netherlands.

00:48:05.040 --> 00:48:06.960
There was a futures market in tulip bulbs

00:48:07.240 --> 00:48:09.640
because obviously you plant them now but you don't know
what's going to come out of the ground.

00:48:09.880 --> 00:48:15.440
So we see already, 400 years ago, that
a money system or a financial system

00:48:15.720 --> 00:48:20.160
is not something that exists in the abstract,
somewhere out there in the ether,

00:48:20.480 --> 00:48:28.080
but something that was to do with states, power,
trade and how they interact with each other.

00:48:30.040 --> 00:48:36.800
Unlike tulips, which are a disposable luxury,
houses are both a necessity and a luxury.

00:48:37.320 --> 00:48:42.200
And as such, they are ideal as a vehicle
for money and bubble creation.

00:48:43.160 --> 00:48:48.480
A dwelling is perhaps the most prized
possession of value most people aspire to.

00:48:51.840 --> 00:48:59.080
Inflating house prices in this way allows a nation
to expand its money supply without affecting inflation data.

00:49:00.360 --> 00:49:06.040
The additional purchasing power created increases
the perceived wealth in relation to other nations

00:49:06.840 --> 00:49:09.400
and thus it creates relative power.

00:49:10.480 --> 00:49:15.920
It is a way of increasing monetary power without
investing in the productive growth of industry.

00:49:18.240 --> 00:49:22.120
Certainly if you look at Britain and America
as outstanding examples of this,

00:49:22.440 --> 00:49:24.960
these are  countries with very
high rates of private home ownership

00:49:25.160 --> 00:49:28.880
so you've got a good base to try and perform
this sort of policy off the back of.

00:49:29.040 --> 00:49:33.920
I think it was quite deliberate in the case
of the US, almost explicit, as Alan Greenspan

00:49:34.200 --> 00:49:41.160
as head of the Federal Reserve when confronted by
a stock market crash at the end of the 1990s

00:49:41.640 --> 00:49:48.360
quite deliberately slashed interest rates to
almost zero. Everyone can borrow very, very cheaply,

00:49:48.640 --> 00:49:52.840
in particular its very easy to borrow
against a house because this is an asset

00:49:53.080 --> 00:49:56.960
and is potentially something that the bank can say Well,
OK we're not just lending you money unsecured,

00:49:57.280 --> 00:50:00.600
you actually do have a house so that's great
because we can repossess it.

00:50:00.760 --> 00:50:03.640
They won't tell you this when you take the
mortgage but they can do this

00:50:03.960 --> 00:50:07.040
and that bubble is then what fuels
expansion such as it is,

00:50:07.160 --> 00:50:12.280
inside the US and inside the UK where something
similar takes place for the next decade or so.

00:50:12.640 --> 00:50:16.480
I think it's also a reflection of an
underlying weakness in these governments

00:50:16.760 --> 00:50:22.760
that they simply lack the will and possibly the ability,
but I think it more comes down to a will,

00:50:23.080 --> 00:50:28.960
to challenge financial markets, to challenge big capital
and say We're going to do something different now.

00:50:29.280 --> 00:50:33.080
And you're going to have to go along with it
because we've been democratically elected

00:50:33.280 --> 00:50:37.240
and you lot frankly haven't and we have a mandate
to do this and we're going to make this happen.

00:50:37.480 --> 00:50:43.400
Just remember it's all part of the plan.
What are you yapping about - you voted for it!

00:50:45.720 --> 00:50:53.040
In Holland or the Netherlands what we had over a period
of trying to get independence initially from Spain

00:50:53.280 --> 00:50:58.480
and trying to raise money to get an army
to free themselves was financial innovation.

00:50:58.720 --> 00:51:03.160
They innovated public lotteries to get money
together. They had public subscription.

00:51:03.400 --> 00:51:08.520
This was the idea that led to the idea of public shares
- a piece of the action that anybody could invest in -

00:51:08.760 --> 00:51:14.440
that meant that something like two thirds of the
population was investing in tulip bulbs by the 1630s.

00:51:14.960 --> 00:51:19.960
After independence these instruments
were applied to financing expansion.

00:51:20.280 --> 00:51:25.120
Why was such a small country able to hold
its own against so much bigger countries

00:51:25.480 --> 00:51:32.640
for example Spain and Portugal that had the benefits
of their empires for over a century in respect of the Netherlands?

00:51:32.960 --> 00:51:35.960
Why could they compete?
On what resource basis?

00:51:36.240 --> 00:51:42.360
Well they had a more efficient, a more evolved
and a broader based financial system

00:51:42.760 --> 00:51:50.680
with these instruments that they'd innovated that allowed them to bring
more money to bear at one point then anybody else, more quickly.

00:51:52.480 --> 00:51:56.400
Incredible But True.

00:51:59.040 --> 00:52:02.400
How to avoid inflation

00:52:03.120 --> 00:52:10.800
Now, inflation can be avoided if the amount
of money that goes into the economy is regulated

00:52:11.120 --> 00:52:17.560
in a way that it doesn't exceed the actual
activity that's happening in the economy.

00:52:18.040 --> 00:52:24.280
Now, the best way to do that, in my opinion,
is to make sure that money is issued into the economy

00:52:24.720 --> 00:52:30.840
only for productive investment,
for productive goods and services,

00:52:31.080 --> 00:52:40.640
so money goes in to help a small business to start
up which creates jobs, which creates additional purchasing power

00:52:41.320 --> 00:52:43.960
which means there's no inflation.

00:52:44.240 --> 00:52:49.720
During their history almost all central banks
have employed forms of direct credit regulation.

00:52:50.320 --> 00:52:57.480
The central bank would determine desired nominal GDP growth then
calculate the necessary amount of credit creation to achieve this.

00:52:58.840 --> 00:53:05.880
And then allocate this credit creation both across the
various banks and type of banks and across the industrial sectors.

00:53:07.040 --> 00:53:15.520
Unproductive credit was suppressed. Thus it was difficult
or impossible to obtain bank credit for large scale,

00:53:15.920 --> 00:53:21.920
purely speculative transactions such as today's
large scale bank funding to hedge funds.

00:53:23.040 --> 00:53:28.840
The World Bank recognised in a 1993 study that
this method of intervention in credit allocation

00:53:29.240 --> 00:53:32.240
was at the core of the East Asian economic miracle.

00:53:32.640 --> 00:53:37.520
There're all sorts of things that governments have done
in the past, very successfully in a number of cases

00:53:37.840 --> 00:53:43.480
and often not unsuccessfully in this country but
the examples that spring to mind like South Korea, Japan,

00:53:43.840 --> 00:53:47.960
often in East Asia where governments have been quite
targeted about how they're going to rebalance the economy,

00:53:48.240 --> 00:53:53.320
picking sectors and deciding where the investment should
take place, I think that has to start happening in the UK

00:53:53.640 --> 00:53:58.560
because we're in a demand side recession
rather than looking at crisis of supply.

00:53:58.840 --> 00:54:05.480
You have to have a system where credit
is put into productive avenues,

00:54:05.840 --> 00:54:08.360
where credit is put into building high speed rail links,

00:54:08.600 --> 00:54:15.720
where credit is put into building houses rather
than giving people money to inflate the price of houses.

00:54:16.040 --> 00:54:24.960
So it's quite simple really in that way and the current
system is simply set up not to do that basically.

00:54:27.120 --> 00:54:32.080
The creation of money by private banks for
non-productive usage causes real inflation

00:54:32.440 --> 00:54:36.520
and as such it is a tax on the purchasing
power of the medium of exchange.

00:54:40.440 --> 00:54:43.720
Decrease in the standard of living

00:54:44.240 --> 00:54:46.920
The figures for the UK are quite stark actually.

00:54:47.240 --> 00:54:56.320
The average median real incomes (for the bit in the middle)
for most people declined over the last 8 years.

00:54:56.640 --> 00:54:59.080
They are now in quite sharp
decline as we go into recession

00:54:59.320 --> 00:55:04.880
- the sharpest really since about the 1930s -
so real income is declining.

00:55:06.440 --> 00:55:10.960
Bank created fiat currency allows the private
banks to suck wealth from the economy

00:55:11.480 --> 00:55:15.120
and over time results in a gradual
decrease in the standard of living.

00:55:16.840 --> 00:55:20.560
As people become poorer
they become even more dependent on debt

00:55:22.040 --> 00:55:25.920
and this at a time when efficiency and
mechanisation have improved dramatically.

00:55:27.160 --> 00:55:33.960
If you go back to the 1960s and we were expected
to, we were looking forward to an age of leisure,

00:55:35.640 --> 00:55:39.880
television programmes saying What are
people going to do with their spare time?

00:55:40.640 --> 00:55:48.280
And now we have got more people working harder
than ever, spending more than ever, which looks great,

00:55:48.640 --> 00:55:54.960
everyone is spending more, but if you're not
actually benefiting from what you're spending,

00:55:55.440 --> 00:56:00.680
if you're having to spend the money on childcare
costs on commuting costs and so forth,

00:56:02.720 --> 00:56:07.520
costs that people didn't in the past used to
have to pay because you could walk to work

00:56:07.840 --> 00:56:15.520
and one member of the family was able to stay at home and be a
permanent homemaker, then you're not actually any better off.

00:56:16.280 --> 00:56:20.640
Everyone is under such enormous pressures nowadays.

00:56:22.120 --> 00:56:28.520
I am conscious that my four nephews and
nieces are facing difficult times.

00:56:29.440 --> 00:56:40.160
They're just going to find themselves having to work very hard
just to keep a roof over their heads, to get a roof over their heads.

00:56:41.600 --> 00:56:45.440
People are getting poorer in real terms.
It's because prices are always going up

00:56:45.720 --> 00:56:49.840
because all this new funny money is being
pumped into the system by the banks

00:56:51.440 --> 00:56:56.480
and they're creating it all as debt so at the same time
as prices are going up and things are getting more expensive,

00:56:56.760 --> 00:56:58.560
we're getting further and further into debt

00:56:58.840 --> 00:57:04.760
and our wealth and the return that we get from actually
working is getting less and less all the time.

00:57:05.040 --> 00:57:09.400
You can't deal with poverty when you have a
financial system and a money system

00:57:09.640 --> 00:57:12.920
that distributes money from
the poor to the very rich.

00:57:13.240 --> 00:57:18.960
Any distribution that you try and do in the opposite
direction is effectively pissing in the wind.

00:57:19.400 --> 00:57:27.760
If you look at issues like increasing inequality one
obvious way to tackle inequality is to have, for example,

00:57:28.040 --> 00:57:31.960
a redistributive tax system. You tax
the rich you give some money to the poor.

00:57:32.240 --> 00:57:34.760
You move a bit of money down the scale.

00:57:36.400 --> 00:57:41.720
That's all very well but if you completely overlook
the fact that there's another redistributive system

00:57:42.240 --> 00:57:47.800
which is taking money from the poor and giving it to the rich,
then you're not really going to tackle this inequality

00:57:48.640 --> 00:57:56.080
and the way a debt-based money system works - it guarantees that
for every pound of money there's going to be a pound of debt.

00:57:56.640 --> 00:58:01.680
That debt is typically going to end up
with the poor, the lower-middle classes,

00:58:02.040 --> 00:58:05.520
those people end up with the debt
and they end up paying interest on that money

00:58:05.800 --> 00:58:12.200
which then goes back to the banking sector and gets distributed
to the people working in the City or in Wall Street.

00:58:12.920 --> 00:58:19.120
What this system does overall is it distributes
money from the poor to the rich essentially,

00:58:19.440 --> 00:58:25.520
distributes money from the poorer regions
of the UK back to the City of London

00:58:25.920 --> 00:58:32.840
and it also distributes money from all the small
businesses, all the little factories around the UK

00:58:33.120 --> 00:58:35.360
and distributes that money back into the financial sector.

00:58:35.960 --> 00:58:42.440
We have a system whereby the activity of
actually supplying our nations money

00:58:42.920 --> 00:58:50.720
occurs under the very same roof as the same organisation that is
responsible for profiting from putting together borrowers and lenders

00:58:51.080 --> 00:58:59.120
i.e. a bank. So, a bank creates our nation's money
supply as well as making loans for profit.

00:58:59.520 --> 00:59:07.120
The government cannot allow the banking system to fail
because if it did over 97% of all money would disappear.

00:59:08.200 --> 00:59:12.880
This is why in the event of a crisis
the risk is transferred to the taxpayer.

00:59:13.720 --> 00:59:19.880
But even during normal times banks receive numerous
guarantees and benefits beyond the right to create money.

00:59:20.400 --> 00:59:23.000
Bill, by the way, I know the Bank of
America is a very big bank,

00:59:23.240 --> 00:59:25.880
it happens that I have $32 there myself.

00:59:26.200 --> 00:59:30.000
Just between us what assurance do
I have that this money is safe?

00:59:30.800 --> 00:59:36.520
Well, all deposits up to $10,000 are insured
by the Federal Government in Washington.

00:59:36.800 --> 00:59:37.800
That's my guarantee?

00:59:38.040 --> 00:59:39.120
Yes sir.

00:59:39.360 --> 00:59:45.320
Have you heard that the Federal Government
is about $280 billion in the hole?

00:59:47.520 --> 00:59:55.120
Banks receive large safety nets from the government.
The taxpayer guarantees 85,000 pounds as deposit insurance.

00:59:55.800 --> 01:00:01.360
And the Bank of England provides liquidity insurance
in case a bank runs out of reserve currency.

01:00:07.120 --> 01:00:15.600
Someone wrote that a big investment bank is like a
Giant Vampire Squid wrapped around the face of humanity.

01:00:16.400 --> 01:00:22.160
Hypnotising politicians. Who throw money
at the banks. No strings attached.

01:00:22.520 --> 01:00:31.120
No matter what damage is done. Trashing the planet.
Forcing cuts to things that make life better.

01:00:31.840 --> 01:00:41.200
Goodbye schools. Goodbye playgrounds.
Goodbye jobs. The bankers that we bailed out

01:00:41.720 --> 01:00:47.640
then gave themselves bonuses that were bigger
than the first wave of public spending cuts.

01:00:48.400 --> 01:00:56.560
Britain alone gave the banks more money than
it cost to put a man on the moon 6 times over.

01:00:57.720 --> 01:01:07.240
Where did our money go? Who let the banks get
away with it? Why? Can Vampire Squids ever be useful?

01:01:08.560 --> 01:01:15.680
No Government yet is brave enough to
tame them perhaps they need a plan.

01:01:20.720 --> 01:01:24.120
Take back our banks

01:01:26.120 --> 01:01:29.480
Ever increasing debt

01:01:34.120 --> 01:01:40.040
The spending cuts agenda is an attempt by the government to
shift debt from its account to that of the public.

01:01:41.520 --> 01:01:47.200
This is the Government's response to the bank bail outs
and is necessary in a debt based monetary system

01:01:47.840 --> 01:01:53.640
where increased purchasing power is the result of
growing debt and where a diversification of debt

01:01:53.920 --> 01:01:57.200
provides overall stability and market confidence.

01:01:58.120 --> 01:02:05.960
Policies such as student fee increases and the privatisation
of public services, assets and industry follow the same model.

01:02:06.560 --> 01:02:13.320
The problem we're facing is that there is this
transference from the public debt to private debt

01:02:13.560 --> 01:02:20.840
which is essentially a way of transferring risk,
away from UK plc and the Government

01:02:21.920 --> 01:02:28.040
on to the heads of individuals and it's going to be the most
vulnerable individuals who are going to have the most debt.

01:02:28.520 --> 01:02:38.720
Thus it's a very regressive policy framework that the Government's
embarking on where the risk is moved on to those who are most vulnerable

01:02:39.120 --> 01:02:42.800
and if there is another financial shock,
if there's an oil shock for example,

01:02:43.120 --> 01:02:46.600
the people who will pay the penalty
are the poorest people in society

01:02:46.920 --> 01:02:53.720
or homeowners for example who will fall into negative
equity if interest rates go up even 1 or 2 percent

01:02:54.320 --> 01:03:00.360
there will be really big problems. So I don't think
it's a sensible way forward for us at the moment at all.

01:03:00.720 --> 01:03:06.680
It's regressive and it's certainly not fair in
the terms that the Government is talking about

01:03:07.000 --> 01:03:10.000
and it's certainly not a case
of We are in this together.

01:03:10.600 --> 01:03:16.520
As more of the country's resources and industries are
privatised the private sector takes on more debt.

01:03:17.120 --> 01:03:20.920
As a result more money is
created and there is a boom.

01:03:22.520 --> 01:03:30.440
Some private equity companies have taken this theory to
the extreme, engaging in a practice known as a Leveraged Buy Out,

01:03:31.320 --> 01:03:38.760
where a company is purchased at an often inflated price and
the purchase price is transferred to the business as a debt.

01:03:40.520 --> 01:03:44.880
The company becomes responsible for
the funding of its own purchase.

01:03:45.200 --> 01:03:52.520
These debts are often so great that the company needs
to reduce staff, salaries and research activities.

01:03:54.200 --> 01:03:56.480
When you have to factor interest as a business,

01:03:56.720 --> 01:04:00.520
if you have to factor interest repayment
into your goods and services,

01:04:00.760 --> 01:04:04.920
then you have to charge a perpetually higher
price as you take on more and more debt.

01:04:05.800 --> 01:04:10.360
An increase in the diversification of debt
results in an increase in the money supply.

01:04:11.920 --> 01:04:20.040
When the money supply increases more money is available for productive
activities and consumption which is the condition for a boom.

01:04:22.240 --> 01:04:24.920
It's questionable whether we're
going to get out of this recession

01:04:25.200 --> 01:04:28.120
or whether we'll just keep ticking
along the way the way that we are now.

01:04:28.800 --> 01:04:35.280
However if we do, then when we come out of  this recession
and when growth starts again look at what happens to debt.

01:04:35.520 --> 01:04:38.960
It will rise and it will keep rising
and the faster the economy is growing,

01:04:39.200 --> 01:04:45.560
the faster the debt will rise and then give it
another 3 to 5 years we'll be back where we were.

01:04:46.200 --> 01:04:49.240
The debt will become too much -
people will start defaulting again.

01:04:49.920 --> 01:04:55.160
It's kind of the system that we're locked into now is that we
can't grow the economy without growing the debt

01:04:55.520 --> 01:04:58.280
and the debt is the very thing that
will bring down the economy.

01:04:58.520 --> 01:05:02.840
The only option going forward is to reform it
to stop banks from creating money as debt.

01:05:04.200 --> 01:05:09.880
By fixing the monetary system we can prevent
the banks from ever causing another financial crisis

01:05:10.200 --> 01:05:16.640
and we can also make the current public service cuts and the
tax rises and the increase in national debt unnecessary.

01:05:18.720 --> 01:05:23.120
The current monetary system allows the banking
sector to extract wealth from the economy,

01:05:23.400 --> 01:05:26.000
whilst providing nothing productive in return.

01:05:27.960 --> 01:05:30.560
Why is it that we've got all this technology,

01:05:31.520 --> 01:05:41.040
all this new efficiency and yet it now requires two people to finance
a household whereas in the 50's it only needed one person working?

01:05:41.600 --> 01:05:46.080
The reason for that is not because these washing
machines and everything are more expensive.

01:05:46.600 --> 01:05:52.560
It's because of all the debt and because the banking
sector is effectively creaming it off from everybody else.

01:05:52.920 --> 01:05:56.760
So a growing banking sector is not a good thing.

01:05:57.200 --> 01:06:03.360
If the banking sector is growing it's either that it's becoming
less efficient or it's becoming a parasite on the rest of the economy.

01:06:04.320 --> 01:06:09.040
We can talk about the banking
sector becoming 4%, 5%, 6% of GDP,

01:06:09.720 --> 01:06:14.280
what's happening to the rest of the economy?
It's becoming 96, 95, 94% of GDP.

01:06:14.600 --> 01:06:21.400
We've got to get switched on to this now. If we want
to have a chance of tackling any of the other big social issues,

01:06:21.400 --> 01:06:23.440
you've got to figure out the money issue.

01:06:23.800 --> 01:06:32.360
The poorest in the world pay for crises even when they've not
benefited from the often reckless and speculative booms,

01:06:32.600 --> 01:06:36.720
like the housing boom in Ireland that preceded that crisis.

01:06:37.120 --> 01:06:45.640
Over the last 30 years we've seen income differentials increase so
that the rich have got much, much richer and ordinary people haven't,

01:06:46.000 --> 01:06:48.320
they've stayed the same or they've got poorer.

01:06:48.720 --> 01:06:52.600
One of the ways that the economy was
kept going was by providing cheap credit,

01:06:52.920 --> 01:06:58.680
providing debt to those very people who couldn't
really afford things anymore, so they kept buying

01:06:59.200 --> 01:07:06.840
and when it collapses it's those same people that have to pay once
again even though in many ways they were the victims the first time.

01:07:08.160 --> 01:07:15.160
As a result of the crisis the Bank of England has bought
corporate debt and repackaged it at lower rates of interest.

01:07:15.720 --> 01:07:21.440
Yet the average person is being asked to pay more
than ever to borrow on overdrafts and credit cards.

01:07:22.320 --> 01:07:28.440
Debts between the very wealthy or between
governments can always be renegotiated

01:07:28.720 --> 01:07:30.760
and always have been throughout world history.

01:07:31.120 --> 01:07:36.240
They're not anything set in stone. It's generally speaking
when you have debts owed by the poor to the rich

01:07:36.520 --> 01:07:40.960
that suddenly debts become a sacred obligation
more important than anything else.

01:07:41.320 --> 01:07:43.600
The idea of renegotiating
them becomes unthinkable.

01:07:45.200 --> 01:07:49.440
Can you pin down exactly what
would keep investors happy,

01:07:49.720 --> 01:07:51.640
make them feel more confident?

01:07:52.920 --> 01:07:58.320
That's a tough one. Personally it doesn't
matter. See I'm a trader -

01:07:58.760 --> 01:08:00.120
I don't really care about that kind of stuff.

01:08:00.400 --> 01:08:01.600
Pay your taxes!

01:08:01.840 --> 01:08:03.320
Were you born in England?

01:08:04.320 --> 01:08:07.280
If I see an opportunity to make money, I go with that.

01:08:07.720 --> 01:08:11.880
For most traders, we don't really care that much
how they're going to fix the economy,

01:08:12.200 --> 01:08:16.920
how they're going to fix the whole situation.
Our job is to make money from it

01:08:17.520 --> 01:08:24.360
and personally I've been dreaming about this  moment for three years.
If you know what to do, you can make a lot of money from this.

01:08:25.120 --> 01:08:28.840
I have a confession which is, I go to bed
every night I dream of another recession,

01:08:29.120 --> 01:08:30.840
I dream of another moment like this.

01:08:31.520 --> 01:08:37.320
I dream of another recession, I dream of another
moment like this. You can make a lot of money from this.

01:08:38.760 --> 01:08:44.960
Bruno, Virginia hurt somebody real bad, you
oughta help her.

01:08:45.320 --> 01:08:47.040
Incoming!

01:08:48.200 --> 01:08:53.840
The way in which you can look across Europe now
and see that the new Prime Minister of Greece,

01:08:54.120 --> 01:08:58.600
not elected, essentially imposed,  Papademos
- former employee of Goldman Sachs.

01:08:58.920 --> 01:09:03.600
The new Prime Minister and Finance Minister  of Italy
- Mario Monti - former employee of Goldman Sachs.

01:09:03.840 --> 01:09:07.640
The new President of the European Central Bank
- former employee of Goldman Sachs.

01:09:08.120 --> 01:09:10.320
You see these people popping up absolutely everywhere.

01:09:10.520 --> 01:09:15.400
That's the way to change what we have, take
all power and all freedoms away from the people

01:09:15.720 --> 01:09:19.800
and collect everything into the hands of one
small group with absolute power.

01:09:21.520 --> 01:09:27.680
From the people, without the people, against the people.

01:09:28.040 --> 01:09:33.960
What's been interesting out of all this is the question
of democracy that's been opened up very starkly in Europe,

01:09:34.320 --> 01:09:37.040
that you have a government of
bankers essentially imposed upon you.

01:09:37.320 --> 01:09:42.840
It's bankers who more or less got us into this mess to put
it rather crudely, but that's a good first approximation

01:09:43.560 --> 01:09:46.240
and then you say OK, Bankers are the people
who therefore are going to get us out of it

01:09:46.520 --> 01:09:51.120
and incidentally there going to run your country now. There's
a serious question of democracy that has opened up here.

01:09:51.920 --> 01:09:59.080
By the way, the banking crisis drove more
than a 100 million people back into poverty.

01:10:01.120 --> 01:10:08.560
The mortality statistics of people who go into
poverty rise hugely for a whole range of reasons.

01:10:09.120 --> 01:10:13.680
So the banking crisis isn't just about becoming
poorer - it was about killing people as well.

01:10:14.600 --> 01:10:18.600
And guess what? We haven't
really got to the bottom of it.

01:10:18.920 --> 01:10:24.440
We never held anybody to account and we haven't done the
radical reforming job that we really needed to do

01:10:24.920 --> 01:10:32.480
because we mistakenly thought If we destabilise
the position any further, it'll make matters worse.

01:10:32.920 --> 01:10:36.880
And guess who took the decisions? All the
people who were there in the first place.

01:10:39.000 --> 01:10:43.240
"I think you ought to know, that the business of
one of these businessmen is murder."

01:10:44.840 --> 01:10:52.000
"Their weapons are modern, their thinking:
two thousand years out of date."

01:10:56.600 --> 01:10:59.280
International aspects

01:10:59.880 --> 01:11:05.200
Look, I was there when the Secretary and the
Chairman of the Federal Reserve came those days

01:11:05.440 --> 01:11:10.520
and talked with members of Congress about
what was going on. It was about September 15th

01:11:10.840 --> 01:11:13.960
Here are the facts, and we don't even talk about these things

01:11:14.560 --> 01:11:22.440
on Thursday at about 11 o'clock in the morning the
Federal Reserve noticed a tremendous draw down

01:11:23.240 --> 01:11:35.320
of money market accounts in the United States to  the tune of
$550 billion was being drawn out in the matter of an hour or two.

01:11:35.760 --> 01:11:43.680
The Treasury opened up its window to help,
they pumped $105 billion into the system

01:11:44.440 --> 01:11:50.480
and quickly realised they could not stem the tide.
We were having an electronic run on the banks.

01:11:50.760 --> 01:12:00.000
They decided to close the operation, close down the money
accounts and announce a guarantee of $250,000 per account

01:12:00.480 --> 01:12:03.320
so there wouldn't be further panic
out there, that's what actually happened.

01:12:03.800 --> 01:12:10.160
If they had not done that their
estimation was by 2 o'clock that afternoon

01:12:11.040 --> 01:12:16.360
$5.5 trillion would have been drawn out of
the money market system of the United States.

01:12:17.240 --> 01:12:23.600
It would have collapsed the entire economy of the United States
and within 24 hours the world economy would have collapsed.

01:12:24.600 --> 01:12:28.760
When money is withdrawn internationally
from one currency to another

01:12:29.240 --> 01:12:35.360
the reserve currency shifts from the national bank of one
country to the reserve account of the foreign bank.

01:12:36.720 --> 01:12:42.160
Foreign banks have relationships with local
banks that allow them to hold foreign reserve currencies

01:12:42.480 --> 01:12:47.520
whilst not being a part of the central
bank scheme at the local central bank.

01:12:51.200 --> 01:12:59.120
For example when 1,000 pounds is transferred
into euros a UK bank will agree an exchange rate

01:12:59.360 --> 01:13:04.760
with a Euro area bank, perhaps
1.15 euros to the pound.

01:13:05.720 --> 01:13:13.480
The UK bank will then transfer £1,000 of the central reserve
currency to the UK partner bank of the European bank

01:13:14.480 --> 01:13:20.040
whilst the European bank will transfer
1,150 euros of reserve currency

01:13:20.520 --> 01:13:23.280
to the European partner bank of the UK bank.

01:13:25.000 --> 01:13:29.160
What happens when currencies and the exchange
rate system is no longer managed,

01:13:29.640 --> 01:13:31.280
what are some of the first consequences?

01:13:32.120 --> 01:13:33.480
Devaluations.

01:13:33.760 --> 01:13:35.400
Speculation.

01:13:35.920 --> 01:13:42.000
Imbalances. Where some countries would accrue
more and more and more of what? What will they accrue?

01:13:42.160 --> 01:13:45.440
Other currencies, other currencies.

01:13:47.840 --> 01:13:54.040
The reserve currency needs to be spent in the country
of origin or exchanged into other currencies.

01:13:54.920 --> 01:14:00.280
Most foreign banks do not have deposit taking
accounts outside of their national borders

01:14:00.520 --> 01:14:06.160
and as such the foreign reserves they hold do
not come back to them in the form of deposits.

01:14:08.600 --> 01:14:14.800
When a country accumulates trade imbalances it
either accumulates foreign reserve currencies

01:14:15.240 --> 01:14:22.400
in the case of surplus or spends its own
reserves in the case of negative trade balances.

01:14:23.560 --> 01:14:30.320
Balance of trade is basically the difference between what
you're selling abroad and what you're buying from abroad.

01:14:31.360 --> 01:14:36.440
Now, the feature of the UK is
that for a very long period of time

01:14:37.400 --> 01:14:39.920
it's had a deficit of something
called a visible balance of trade

01:14:40.160 --> 01:14:42.120
which is trading things that you can see.

01:14:42.240 --> 01:14:48.840
So that is goods that you'd recognise, stuff you can put in
containers, it's cars, computers, things that you'd see in a shop.

01:14:49.240 --> 01:14:57.800
That's been a substantial deficit. I think it opened up in
the early 1980s and essentially it hasn't gone away since

01:14:58.200 --> 01:15:00.240
- if anything it's got wider and wider.

01:15:00.600 --> 01:15:05.160
Foreign exchange reserves cannot be
directly used for domestic spending.

01:15:06.080 --> 01:15:10.400
The money can only be spent abroad or on imports.

01:15:12.760 --> 01:15:21.200
A country with a large balance of trade deficit relies on its
creditors to spend the imbalances accrued in its own market.

01:15:22.360 --> 01:15:29.080
There have been proposals in the past to try and
create a mechanism for those imbalances to match up.

01:15:29.520 --> 01:15:33.680
For instance John Maynard Keynes
at the end of the Second World War

01:15:33.840 --> 01:15:40.600
- his original proposal for what became Bretton Woods and
the set of institutions settled there like the IMF and World Bank

01:15:40.920 --> 01:15:43.200
was that there would be a kind of
international clearing union.

01:15:43.720 --> 01:15:48.240
This particularly related to the trade side
rather than the financial side directly

01:15:48.520 --> 01:15:54.440
but the principle was that once trade balances had opened
up everybody would bank through an international clearing bank

01:15:54.920 --> 01:16:01.240
and that would kind of force everyone to eventually reconcile
the imbalances that appeared in the real economy.

01:16:01.720 --> 01:16:11.320
But no such mechanism exists. The accumulated
net trade imbalance of the UK is around 800 billion pounds.

01:16:15.320 --> 01:16:18.720
Currency wars

01:16:20.160 --> 01:16:26.960
In essence what has happened is that over many years some countries
have had big trade surpluses and others big trade deficits.

01:16:27.520 --> 01:16:31.120
The countries with trade deficits have been
spending more than they've been earning

01:16:31.440 --> 01:16:36.200
so they've had to borrow from abroad and
they've been doing this year after year.

01:16:36.520 --> 01:16:41.960
Countries like that, the United States,
ourselves and some other countries in Europe

01:16:42.320 --> 01:16:46.120
- that cannot go on and there are two ways
in which this can come to an end.

01:16:46.520 --> 01:16:52.240
Either and we've seen this in some of the countries in
Europe, if they can't find new ways to become competitive

01:16:52.520 --> 01:16:55.680
then their ability to repay
the debts is called into question.

01:16:56.120 --> 01:17:01.160
Another way of doing it, which we followed is
that we have a credible plan to repay our debts

01:17:01.520 --> 01:17:08.440
and the value of sterling has fallen by 25% to make our
exports more competitive and attractive to overseas buyers

01:17:09.120 --> 01:17:15.080
and to be more attractive for British consumers to buy
from British producers rather than overseas producers.

01:17:15.520 --> 01:17:21.960
That is what we have done to put in place a framework to
rebalance our economy and I'm sure that's the right way to do it.

01:17:22.800 --> 01:17:30.160
Currency war, also known as competitive devaluation,
is a condition where countries compete against each other

01:17:30.480 --> 01:17:33.640
to achieve a relatively low
exchange rate for their currency.

01:17:34.320 --> 01:17:40.520
As the price to buy a particular currency falls so too
does the real price of exports from that country.

01:17:41.880 --> 01:17:46.480
Domestic industry receives a boost
in demand both at home and abroad.

01:17:48.080 --> 01:17:52.440
It's made British exports appear rather
cheaper so they recovered a little bit

01:17:52.680 --> 01:17:56.240
but because the rest of the world is looking really
quite ropey they've started to fall back down again.

01:17:56.480 --> 01:18:02.600
So what we're looking at is something that is almost like
a kind of anarchy and in a way an increasing anarchy.

01:18:02.880 --> 01:18:08.080
This is  what's happened over the last few years where the
Brazilian Finance Minister has been the most vocal about this,

01:18:08.480 --> 01:18:15.600
talking about currency wars, talking about the desire of
national governments when confronted by a major recession

01:18:15.880 --> 01:18:19.840
they think If we could export more we
can dig ourselves out of this recession.

01:18:20.080 --> 01:18:22.840
If we want to export more
we depreciate our currency.

01:18:23.200 --> 01:18:26.760
That makes our goods cheaper everyone else
buys them and we'll all be better off.

01:18:27.160 --> 01:18:31.400
The issue here is if you depreciate its like
everyone else appreciates against you.

01:18:31.680 --> 01:18:34.600
Their stuff becomes more expensive
so they're not happy about that.

01:18:34.880 --> 01:18:40.000
They also want to depreciate and this is where you can
see a competitive round of devaluations breaking out.

01:18:40.680 --> 01:18:48.320
To decrease the value of its national currency a national
central bank sells reserve currency into the market.

01:18:48.760 --> 01:18:53.280
It creates this currency out of nothing
by typing numbers into a computer.

01:18:53.520 --> 01:18:57.680
i.e. a central bank buys foreign reserve currency.

01:18:58.040 --> 01:19:06.840
The amount a central bank can create is not limited
because there is no defined commodity behind
the reserve currency.

01:19:07.280 --> 01:19:12.520
During the long phase of commodity money, the
exchange rate would depend on the amount of gold,

01:19:12.880 --> 01:19:17.040
silver or copper contained
in the coins of each country.

01:19:17.480 --> 01:19:22.320
Similarly after the advent of paper
 money and the gold standard,

01:19:22.880 --> 01:19:28.240
the exchange rate depended on the amount of gold the
government promised to pay the holder of the bank notes.

01:19:28.480 --> 01:19:36.120
These amounts did not vary greatly in the short term and as
such exchange rates between currencies were relatively stable.

01:19:38.640 --> 01:19:49.000
After the Second World War currencies were pegged to the dollar
and the dollar was backed by gold, this system came to an end in 1971.

01:19:49.240 --> 01:19:55.200
So, we have a modern financial system where
money is now chaotically organised,

01:19:55.440 --> 01:19:59.760
there is no exchange rate because there
is no gold standard system to sustain it,

01:20:00.040 --> 01:20:04.160
so we don't need it. In fact we believe the
market will resolve all the problems of exchange

01:20:04.440 --> 01:20:09.360
whether your currency should be worth more than mine
is a reflection of your economy relative to mine

01:20:09.640 --> 01:20:14.160
and if that changes the currency and exchange rate
can change and if we need that to happen

01:20:14.440 --> 01:20:20.080
it will happen magically by the efficiency of market and
profit seeking. You guys know the rest I think.

01:20:21.640 --> 01:20:26.160
A currency's value in relation to another
currency is determined by the market.

01:20:27.040 --> 01:20:31.600
If more people want to buy a currency
than sell it its value increases.

01:20:32.440 --> 01:20:36.320
If more people want to sell, its value decreases.

01:20:37.040 --> 01:20:44.360
The value is set by individual banks as they buy and
sell currencies they will adjust the exchange rate.

01:20:44.840 --> 01:20:55.640
The last study I read in 2007 each day on
currency markets $3.2 trillion are traded, each day.

01:20:57.840 --> 01:21:00.680
Who knows what the global GDP is?

01:21:01.840 --> 01:21:04.680
$50 trillion 50? Again Brucey, higher!

01:21:05.440 --> 01:21:11.680
60; that's closer. The point is - think
about that exchange happening every single day

01:21:12.040 --> 01:21:14.680
- there's about 260 business days a year.

01:21:15.240 --> 01:21:23.200
It takes a few weeks to match the global value of every
economic transaction that happens everywhere,

01:21:23.640 --> 01:21:27.400
every day, in a year. It takes a few weeks.

01:21:27.760 --> 01:21:33.040
Obviously all of us trade currency fairly regularly.
If you go abroad you exchange into another currency.

01:21:33.280 --> 01:21:39.360
That's a form of currency trading - you're swapping
your pounds or euros or yen whatever it might be.

01:21:39.600 --> 01:21:43.360
That happens fairly regularly and that's a
conventional part of the trading process.

01:21:43.600 --> 01:21:46.800
Large corporations have to do this on a regular basis.

01:21:47.000 --> 01:21:52.800
Where it becomes something that people question and where
you get people saying Well hang on, this is speculation!

01:21:53.080 --> 01:21:58.000
is when you get people realising that currencies move around next to
each other and if they move around in value next to each other

01:21:58.320 --> 01:22:03.360
there's always an opportunity to try and make money out of
those changes in value and therefore you can speculate on it.

01:22:03.600 --> 01:22:06.400
That's the more questionable end of the market,

01:22:06.680 --> 01:22:11.280
that's the bit of the market that things like a
financial transactions tax will try and chop away at

01:22:11.600 --> 01:22:17.520
because the assumption there and it's kind of not incorrect
is that it just produces instability for everyone else.

01:22:17.800 --> 01:22:21.040
These people want volatility in the market
because that's how they make their money.

01:22:21.320 --> 01:22:26.240
They want to encourage it and they do encourage it by
trading and speculating in the way that they do.

01:22:27.000 --> 01:22:34.360
By 2010 the foreign exchange market had grown
to be the largest and most liquid market in the world

01:22:35.200 --> 01:22:40.160
with an average of $4 trillion
of currency being exchanged every day.

01:22:43.400 --> 01:22:51.080
Volatility creates a need. What does it do to countries,
especially perhaps small ones like developing countries,

01:22:51.400 --> 01:23:00.280
if there are suddenly huge and instantly fluctuating
financial flows? What do they have to do to cope?

01:23:00.600 --> 01:23:05.320
Increase the production of the products
they're selling and sell more

01:23:05.600 --> 01:23:07.600
Lowering the price

01:23:08.200 --> 01:23:11.200
And becoming possibly even poorer.

01:23:11.560 --> 01:23:17.560
Once you start talking about the international system
it becomes really quite a peculiar thing

01:23:17.800 --> 01:23:23.080
in that a lot of it depends on simply sentiment
and beliefs about what an economy is like

01:23:23.400 --> 01:23:26.520
rather more than it depends on anything the
economy might or might not actually be doing

01:23:26.800 --> 01:23:32.120
and that can shift very rapidly because if it's just
someone's belief about a currency is supportable

01:23:32.600 --> 01:23:35.480
then you know they can carry on
believing this until whenever

01:23:35.800 --> 01:23:39.440
- If that belief changes it can change
very rapidly in a financial market.

01:23:39.760 --> 01:23:45.080
The process of financial contagion can take
place in just minutes  or seconds even.

01:23:45.360 --> 01:23:54.800
You can just move from being an apparently quite a stable robust economy
to being one that suddenly sentiment has turned against you

01:23:54.960 --> 01:23:57.360
and you find that the markets are picking on you.

01:23:57.680 --> 01:24:03.720
It can often be not much more than you're simply the next
door neighbour of a country that's currently in trouble.

01:24:06.080 --> 01:24:10.760
Many of the world's financial crises in
the past thirty years have been caused by

01:24:11.160 --> 01:24:16.200
rapid withdrawals of a nation's currency
or the currencies of an entire region.

01:24:16.760 --> 01:24:21.000
This type of activity is often
referred to as financial warfare.

01:24:23.360 --> 01:24:29.800
It's benefited major institutions really quite
substantially, like Goldman Sachs for example,

01:24:30.080 --> 01:24:36.960
or any large bank has done somewhat better out of this set of arrangements
than it would have done in a far more regulated environment.

01:24:37.200 --> 01:24:42.600
It's made people very, very wealthy. It's allowed
financial markets to expand absolutely enormously.

01:24:42.960 --> 01:24:48.400
Anybody involved in that is keen on seeing a deregulated world.
In the case of the UK you have a government

01:24:48.640 --> 01:24:52.960
which has been quite overtly and
deliberately and aggressively arguing

01:24:53.160 --> 01:24:57.440
against any forms of regulation being
imposed on those financial markets.

01:24:57.760 --> 01:25:00.640
But it's not the case that there's
someone behind the scenes pulling the strings

01:25:00.960 --> 01:25:06.560
- this is how things work - quite deliberately, overtly,
in front of you. That's the world as it is.

01:25:06.880 --> 01:25:09.840
It is making some people very rich.
They're quite happy with it.

01:25:11.920 --> 01:25:15.240
I think it is a form of economic warfare.

01:25:15.760 --> 01:25:23.640
Much of the change in the way that the global economy works
over the last thirty years result from this debt,

01:25:23.960 --> 01:25:31.760
this third world debt because it's given rich countries and banks
and the financial sector enormous amounts of power and control

01:25:32.160 --> 01:25:36.680
over the poorer bits of the world where a lot
of the resources are that we like using

01:25:37.160 --> 01:25:42.600
and that's being used in a way that many people
have compared to a form of colonialism.

01:25:42.880 --> 01:25:49.520
It's a very real direct form of power that's being used
over those countries to force those countries to do

01:25:49.840 --> 01:25:54.280
what are really in the interests of the richest
segments of the world that they do.

01:25:54.520 --> 01:25:59.920
And as a result of that not only
have corporations become absolutely huge,

01:26:00.320 --> 01:26:05.480
made huge amounts of profit and absolutely
enormous and all pervasive,

01:26:05.760 --> 01:26:12.800
but the financial sector has become even bigger than
that and the real money to be made in the world today

01:26:13.080 --> 01:26:19.120
is not by producing anything at all its purely by
forms of speculating. Making money from money

01:26:19.520 --> 01:26:28.640
- that's the most profitable and by far and away the biggest
form of economic activity that exists in the world today.

01:26:29.320 --> 01:26:34.760
To protect themselves, vulnerable countries
need to accrue currency from rich countries

01:26:35.120 --> 01:26:37.840
who create these currencies out of nothing.

01:26:39.320 --> 01:26:43.920
The Netherlands, first Governor General of
Indonesia the man who built the trade routes,

01:26:44.320 --> 01:26:50.560
fortified them, what I mean by that is built forts along
them and fought Spanish fleets and British fleets,

01:26:50.920 --> 01:27:02.600
said about the development of the Netherlands Empire and Netherlands
trade was 'We cannot make trade without war, nor war without trade.'

01:27:04.520 --> 01:27:06.120
Money and power.

01:27:08.120 --> 01:27:11.400
Financial Imperialism

01:27:12.760 --> 01:27:17.640
So reserves have become the way in which you
can insure yourself against what?

01:27:17.920 --> 01:27:26.040
Speculation. Speculative attack.
Falling markets. Bubbles.

01:27:27.520 --> 01:27:32.920
When a country succumbs to a speculative attack
it is asked to deregulate its markets

01:27:33.200 --> 01:27:37.640
and conform its financial system
to that of the dominant party.

01:27:39.680 --> 01:27:43.960
The big problem that's faced by most developing
countries who've got into a debt crises was

01:27:44.280 --> 01:27:48.320
that they were told by the powers that
be in the world, the International Monetary Fund,

01:27:48.640 --> 01:27:57.280
which in many ways governs the global financial system, that the
way to get out of debt is first of all to restructure your economy.

01:27:57.560 --> 01:28:03.080
especially to increase your exports so you're earning
more dollars and then you can pay off your debt

01:28:03.360 --> 01:28:06.400
which is normally in dollars or
some other foreign currency.

01:28:06.880 --> 01:28:10.840
Unfortunately time and time again that
was proved to not be the case at all.

01:28:11.080 --> 01:28:18.520
Actually countries cut back their public spending to the bone so
they stopped growing; they stopped having any potential for growth

01:28:18.920 --> 01:28:23.960
and what they did produce was aimed at the export
market, was aimed at creating dollars and so on.

01:28:24.200 --> 01:28:28.480
They were paying off their debts but
they weren't developing their own economy at all.

01:28:28.800 --> 01:28:33.360
They were paying far more in debt repayments than they were
spending on health or education or anything else and

01:28:33.680 --> 01:28:36.160
their debts just kept getting bigger and bigger.

01:28:36.640 --> 01:28:43.880
The country becomes a vassal state allowing large corporations
to exploit its natural resources and workforce.

01:28:44.240 --> 01:28:49.560
Financial Imperialism: Expanding and maintaining
imperial power through monetary dominance.

01:28:49.920 --> 01:28:54.880
It's not even shadowy. There's no great mystery about
what's happening here and how the world operates.

01:28:55.120 --> 01:29:04.160
It's quite blunt. For the last thirty years you've got something
pretty much everywhere that generally gets labelled Neo-Liberalism

01:29:04.520 --> 01:29:10.480
- this idea that you should have floating exchange rates,
weak regulation particularly of financial markets,

01:29:10.720 --> 01:29:16.080
minimal government interference or involvement in what the
market does and it's more or less how the world operates.

01:29:16.360 --> 01:29:22.720
And then there are institutions - the outstanding one at this point
is the IMF - that will actively try and enforce this state of affairs.

01:29:23.080 --> 01:29:30.680
So it's not greatly shadowy, that there are people behind the scenes
somewhere trying to manipulate stuff, this is actually quite overt.

01:29:30.920 --> 01:29:36.240
This is happening and this is how it
has been for my entire adult life.

01:29:37.680 --> 01:29:43.720
This is how the world is operated and it's  made some people
very wealthy, it's produced enormous concentrations of wealth.

01:29:44.040 --> 01:29:52.240
So when the International Monetary Fund comes in, in order to try
and alleviate a countries debt problems, it imposes a set of conditions.

01:29:52.560 --> 01:29:57.840
In the 1980s and 90's they called that set of
conditions a Structural Adjustment Programme

01:29:58.120 --> 01:30:00.440
and it tends to take very similar
forms wherever it happens.

01:30:00.720 --> 01:30:06.600
Indeed we can see structural adjustment programmes in essence
happening today in countries like Greece and Portugal and Ireland

01:30:07.240 --> 01:30:14.480
where countries are instructed to decrease
the amount they spend on the public sector,

01:30:15.840 --> 01:30:22.240
they are instructed to liberalise their trade market
and liberalise their capital market

01:30:22.640 --> 01:30:25.720
so money can much more easily come
in and out of their economy.

01:30:26.040 --> 01:30:30.680
The idea is that this will encourage investment
to come in from richer parts of the world

01:30:31.040 --> 01:30:34.040
and that all of their problems
will be solved from this investment.

01:30:34.320 --> 01:30:39.920
In actual fact this is proved time and time
again to be completely without foundation.

01:30:40.240 --> 01:30:45.640
In actual fact what happens is it destroys fledgling
industries and capacities in these developing countries

01:30:45.960 --> 01:30:50.640
and developing countries become completely dependent
on goods and services from developed countries

01:30:51.000 --> 01:30:57.400
and also from capital from developed countries. One of the
things the International Monetary Fund is very keen on

01:30:57.840 --> 01:31:04.240
is telling countries to lower the taxes that should be paid by
multinational corporations when they come and operate in a country

01:31:04.560 --> 01:31:07.320
because then you'll encourage
more multinationals to come in.

01:31:07.520 --> 01:31:13.440
Of course what it also means is the profits that are made
by those multinational corporations leave those countries just as quickly

01:31:13.640 --> 01:31:19.680
 - the country itself doesn't benefit. Today you have
many developing countries which have got almost no tax base.

01:31:20.240 --> 01:31:25.840
They've not developed a tax base at all and so they're
even more dependent on international capital markets,

01:31:26.040 --> 01:31:31.840
on the money markets, on creating debt and that's why you
have so many countries in the world that have really been robbed

01:31:32.120 --> 01:31:38.240
of their sovereignty, and it's very difficult to see how democratic
societies can evolve or function when actually a government is

01:31:38.480 --> 01:31:44.560
more dependent on the diktats of the International Monetary
Fund and the money markets than it is on their own people.

01:31:47.000 --> 01:31:50.240
Financial instruments

01:31:51.000 --> 01:32:00.200
What we've seen since the 1970s is a dramatic increase in
a series of phenomena that have had a stimulative effect

01:32:00.560 --> 01:32:07.280
on the changes in the financial system that have brought us
to the gleaming and shiny metal and steel business that's over there.

01:32:07.600 --> 01:32:10.280
In case you don't know that's the
City of London I'm pointing at.

01:32:10.800 --> 01:32:16.160
To compensate for the lack of a defined commodity
based value underlying currencies,

01:32:16.600 --> 01:32:22.760
financial institutions developed
securitisation as a means to manage risk.

01:32:23.600 --> 01:32:28.200
You develop securitisation as a means to
try and stabilise the whole system

01:32:28.480 --> 01:32:34.920
this is a set of financial processes and financial innovations
that really accelerate from the seventies, eighties onwards.

01:32:35.360 --> 01:32:39.880
You had a chaotic system that needed
to manage risk and you had to innovate.

01:32:40.200 --> 01:32:47.480
You needed derivatives, options, futures. You have
new markets in volatility management tools.

01:32:47.760 --> 01:32:49.560
Who knows what the term hedging is?

01:32:50.000 --> 01:32:53.680
Spreading your risk. Managing your risk,
insuring against it, precisely.

01:32:54.600 --> 01:33:00.800
Up until very recently, until the 1960s the Securities
and Exchange Commission would be quite clear

01:33:01.160 --> 01:33:07.600
that derivatives that weren't based on real products like
agricultural products - so pork belly futures or whatever -

01:33:07.960 --> 01:33:11.600
would in fact be essentially a kind of gambling
and therefore you weren't allowed to trade them.

01:33:11.840 --> 01:33:16.800
That changes in the sixties.
Everyone can trade currency futures,

01:33:17.200 --> 01:33:23.440
things that are not based on real products being traded at some
point in the future but are based on the movement of currency prices.

01:33:24.000 --> 01:33:28.800
Once you have the system of fixed exchange rates
breaking down obviously this accelerates enormously

01:33:29.120 --> 01:33:34.800
so as you get the rollback of government regulation here,
you get the market taking over with its own products here

01:33:35.160 --> 01:33:38.360
and the theory is that the market is
 better at regulating itself,

01:33:38.680 --> 01:33:41.160
its more stable than if you have a
government interfering all the time.

01:33:41.520 --> 01:33:46.360
The efficient markets hypothesis - the idea that
you have set up a financial market, they're fast,

01:33:46.720 --> 01:33:50.560
everybody in them is well informed, they all keep a
very careful eye on what everyone else is doing

01:33:50.960 --> 01:33:55.160
 - it'll therefore be very stable and
reflect real changes in the economy.

01:33:55.480 --> 01:34:01.360
It's not going to be driven by panics, manias, speculative
bubbles. None of this is really going to happen.

01:34:01.760 --> 01:34:08.560
If there is movement up and down it's because something real is happening
and traders and investors in financial markets are responding to it.

01:34:08.880 --> 01:34:14.760
So that's the efficient markets hypothesis. The practice
- I think what you see in 2008 is the end of that process -

01:34:15.160 --> 01:34:22.960
the appearance of a crisis so major that belief that it'll simply
be self-stabilising and self-regulating really can't carry on.

01:34:23.320 --> 01:34:26.160
The practice carries on anyway but you can't
really argue in the same way that you used to

01:34:26.520 --> 01:34:29.600
It's good or It's necessary
or This is OK for the world.

01:34:33.840 --> 01:34:37.160
In the last decade we had a new innovation
- something called a credit default swap.

01:34:37.520 --> 01:34:40.360
A way of buying insurance against
a company you invested in going bust

01:34:41.840 --> 01:34:50.880
and in 2002 they were worth less than $1 trillion.
In 2007 they were worth $60 trillion.

01:34:52.440 --> 01:34:54.320
That's five years.

01:34:54.600 --> 01:35:02.520
Everybody is suddenly sitting there saying Oh! These CDO's we've
made don't in fact provide the kind of stability that we thought.

01:35:02.880 --> 01:35:06.600
The maths that's inside of them
is complete nonsense it turns out.

01:35:07.440 --> 01:35:12.880
There's far more risk attached to trying
to securitise risk and securitise debt

01:35:13.240 --> 01:35:17.680
in the way that we have done this than we thought.
And we now think these things are now worthless!

01:35:20.000 --> 01:35:24.960
The attempt to get more and more complex
ways of regulating and shaping a financial market

01:35:25.240 --> 01:35:32.560
and trying to make a quick buck out of it as well actually
helped produce the opposite effect to what its apologists said

01:35:32.840 --> 01:35:35.440
- which is, it led to a spectacular crash.

01:35:39.840 --> 01:35:46.480
What we saw as a result of this very different
situation was one phenomenon above all,

01:35:46.840 --> 01:35:51.440
one sector above all grew, and
that was the financial sector.

01:35:52.640 --> 01:35:56.840
While the financial sector benefits
enormously from the current monetary system,

01:35:57.240 --> 01:35:59.520
the system is neither stable nor fair.

01:36:00.040 --> 01:36:08.720
The assumption in what the Bank of England does right now
is that the cash that we hold is backed up by government debt.

01:36:09.040 --> 01:36:13.080
The government can back up its promises by
the fact that it can tax the public.

01:36:13.640 --> 01:36:17.440
So what they're implying is that
cash is backed up by government debt,

01:36:17.640 --> 01:36:21.480
when government debt is backed up by the
ability of the government to get cash from the public.

01:36:21.840 --> 01:36:28.880
Time and time again over the past thirty years we've seen
private debts being transformed into public debts,

01:36:29.240 --> 01:36:35.720
and ultimately the price of that debt is being
paid by the public in the debtor country.

01:36:37.840 --> 01:36:40.080
This is why spending cuts are necessary.

01:36:41.040 --> 01:36:47.880
The system is designed to make certain people very rich at
the expense of a nation's citizens and tax payers.

01:36:49.600 --> 01:36:55.360
The system lowers the standard of living of the majority
and distributes this wealth amongst the privileged.

01:36:57.400 --> 01:37:03.440
So what we are left with is a financial system since
the early seventies that has no fixed exchange rates

01:37:03.920 --> 01:37:07.880
that suddenly has increasingly open financial borders,

01:37:09.000 --> 01:37:17.240
that has central banks having to manage without having any control
because there's nothing here where the gold used to be.

01:37:18.400 --> 01:37:25.840
Chaotically they have to ease quantitatively.
They have to lend as a lender of last resort.

01:37:27.000 --> 01:37:33.600
Throughout history monetary systems were designed
to give the dominant international power an advantage

01:37:34.600 --> 01:37:37.760
and this power is fiercely
defended and expanded on.

01:37:41.480 --> 01:37:44.880
International currency reform

01:37:45.440 --> 01:37:55.160
What I would like to see is a new kind of currency
that is backed by something that is scarce

01:37:55.440 --> 01:38:01.160
and that we really need and we really value.
Something like energy or renewable energy, for example,

01:38:02.040 --> 01:38:06.680
a kilowatt hour backed currency
would be very interesting to me.

01:38:07.040 --> 01:38:15.080
We need to start valuing things that are most scarce and
that we need to survive as a human race in the long run.

01:38:15.640 --> 01:38:21.120
Backing an international currency with something like
that will generate enormous investment in,

01:38:21.440 --> 01:38:30.720
for example, renewable energy, if that's the primary
international unit of account that is being used.

01:38:31.440 --> 01:38:38.360
Another option is a basket of currencies so
you mix up the value of different currencies

01:38:39.720 --> 01:38:43.760
to create a very solid currency
that people have confidence in.

01:38:45.840 --> 01:38:51.600
Perhaps even better would be a basket of commodities
with which to back up international currencies.

01:38:53.120 --> 01:39:00.800
Now if it was possible, internationally, some way or another,
to get all these increasingly competing national economies together

01:39:01.240 --> 01:39:06.440
and say We're all going to sit down and write out an
agreement, somewhat like the Bretton Woods agreement

01:39:06.720 --> 01:39:13.160
which will allow for, unlike Bretton Woods, some
currencies to be pegged against different baskets of goods

01:39:13.440 --> 01:39:21.120
more appropriate to their national economies. If you could
arrange for that to happen then that would be nice

01:39:21.360 --> 01:39:26.440
and you can see how that would start to create a kind of order
in the international macro economy which is otherwise lacking.

01:39:26.760 --> 01:39:30.640
The real difficulty there is just political
who on Earth is going to do this?

01:39:30.960 --> 01:39:34.800
Who is the force that is
going to make this thing happen?

01:39:36.040 --> 01:39:42.200
Creating a monetary system which is both fair
and stable is possible and can be achieved.

01:39:43.640 --> 01:39:50.400
What are international organisations
for if not for such a purpose?

01:39:53.760 --> 01:39:59.000
Part of the voiceover for this documentary was taken
from the book, "Where does money come from."

01:40:01.280 --> 01:40:10.360
This is George. George worked in a
big bank in the City of London.

01:40:11.320 --> 01:40:18.640
But one day without warning George's bank went bust.
Luckily, the government rescued the bank

01:40:18.840 --> 01:40:24.200
and George kept his job but the greedy government
wanted something in return for their help.

01:40:24.960 --> 01:40:31.240
They demanded a higher tax on George's salary and bonus.
For someone with a high cost lifestyle like George,

01:40:31.480 --> 01:40:39.360
a shock like this can be devastating. Now George struggles to
afford the rent on his riverside apartment in central London.

01:40:39.720 --> 01:40:43.720
The tyres on his Aston Martin are
wearing thin and are barely road legal.

01:40:44.080 --> 01:40:48.760
Unless George's situation improves
- or unless someone like you helps him -

01:40:49.000 --> 01:40:56.200
then George may even be forced to walk to past the next
Saville Row tailors and buy his suit from Topshop or Next.

01:40:57.480 --> 01:41:04.000
Even if George had anything to celebrate he can
no longer afford the champagne to celebrate with.

01:41:04.440 --> 01:41:13.480
George is not alone. Countless others are suffering like him.
No-one knows how long it'll be until the good times return.

01:41:16.360 --> 01:41:27.120
But with your help George can turn his life around. A simple monthly
donation from you can bring a bit of sunshine back to George's life.

01:41:28.920 --> 01:41:34.680
Just £395 will help him celebrate minor
achievements with a magnum of Cristal champagne.

01:41:35.680 --> 01:41:40.200
As little as £900 will help George buy
a new set of tyres for his Aston Martin.

01:41:41.360 --> 01:41:51.040
£2000 can help George recover his self-esteem with a suit from a
prestigious Saville Row tailor. But even a small amount will help.

01:41:52.040 --> 01:41:59.960
Just £200 will buy a meal for George and his girlfriend
Experience. Just £200 extra will buy the drinks.

01:42:01.400 --> 01:42:05.680
By adopting a banker you won't just be
supporting someone like George in a time of need

01:42:06.840 --> 01:42:14.560
- you'll also be supporting the trendy wine bars of the City of London,
the luxury car makers of Italy and the tailors of Saville Row.

01:42:15.040 --> 01:42:20.480
You'll be doing your patriotic duty to support
Britain's greatest industry in its time of need.

01:42:21.920 --> 01:42:24.600
And when the good times return
and George gets his bonus back,

01:42:25.120 --> 01:42:29.240
the taxes he pays will help fund the public
services that the rest of you scroungers depend on.

01:42:29.560 --> 01:42:39.760
So please, until the good times return for
George and those like him, will you give today?

